ll rights reserved.\nLinkedIn Ireland Unlimited Company, Gardner House, Wilton Plaza, Wilton Place,\nDublin 2, Ireland.\nLinkedIn and the LinkedIn logo are registered trademarks of LinkedIn.\n\n \n\n[https://t1.em.linkedin.com/r/?id=hc33212e8-021a-4889-991e-4933438cf81c,7dfe80,1]','incoming-complaint','INCOMING',NULL,'2026-06-21 16:52:16',NULL,NULL,NULL),('a5c56d0c-6633-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7','d69e6b3d-662d-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Dear LinkedIn,\n\nWe have received your ticket regarding: \"Complete your profile\".\n\nYour reference ID is #d69e6b3d. This issue has been logged and is currently under review by our team.','approved-draft-placeholder','INCOMING',NULL,'2026-06-21 16:52:16',NULL,NULL,NULL),('a5d45242-6633-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7','d69e6b3d-662d-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'The Atlas\n\nBy GenAI Works\n\n\nBy GenAI Works\n\n    \n    \n    Read this article on LinkedIn to join the conversation\nRead on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/image-model-thinks-genai-works-ajy9f?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3B4Har7%2BmKR5CsyFgeHj%2B6SQ%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=0oKcZT2wmuUIc1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_-null-icq8ji~moafax34~uc-null-null&eid=icq8ji-moafax34-uc\n  \nWelcome back. OpenAI has pushed Nano Banana off the top with an image model that plans, searches the web and checks its own work before generating. Google has turned Gemini 3.1 Pro into a research agent that ships with PitchBook and S&P plugged in. Meta is recording its own employees to train AI agents. And SpaceX has put a $60 billion option on Cursor. In today\'s Generative AI Newsletter: OpenAI pushes Nano Banana off the image throne with a model that thinks. Google wires PitchBook and FactSet into its new research agent. Meta is logging employee keystrokes to train AI, with no opt-out. SpaceX lines up a $60 billion option on Cursor. OpenAI\'s new image model plans before it paints OpenAI released ChatGPT Images 2.0 today. The model plans, searches the web for reference material and self-checks its outputs before generating. It swept Arena\'s text-to-image leaderboard by a wide margin, ending the long run Nano Banana 2 held at the top. Resolution and throughput: Up to 2K resolution, eight images per prompt, aspect ratios from 3:1 ultrawide to 1:3 tall. Text rendering: Multilingual and handles fine-grained small text that previous models mangled. Thinking mode: Live web lookups for references before generating. Paid plans only. Access: ChatGPT, Codex and the gpt-image-2 API. Altman called it \"GPT-3 to GPT-5 in one release.\" Every leading image model until now has been one-shot: prompt in, image out. Images 2.0 separates planning from rendering, which is why text inside images finally looks like text. For anyone producing visual creative at scale, this closes the last gap between AI output and production-ready design. The Nano Banana era ends quickly. The resume gap costing you interviews Most of the work happens before you even hear back. Your resume has to pass filters, sound human, and match each role. That’s a lot to manage on your own. For the next 7 days, you get full access to Kickresume. Free. No credit card. Here’s what you can actually do with it: Write stronger resume bullets: AI turns your real experience into clear, relevant impact See why you’re getting filtered out: ATS checker shows exact gaps Tailor every application fast: one click, role-specific edits Prep for interviews: questions based on your resume and target role Get unstuck: guidance when you’re unsure what to apply for next This is about removing guesswork and saving time.  You focus on applying. The system handles the heavy lifting. Start your free 7-day access Google\'s research agent prices the analyst by the prompt Google launched Deep Research and Deep Research Max this morning. Both run on Gemini 3.1 Pro inside NotebookLM and generate research reports from the web, uploaded files or any Model Context Protocol server. Benchmarks claim a meaningful jump on retrieval and reasoning over Opus 4.6 and GPT 5.4. Same engine: The backbone that powered NotebookLM is now an agent, replacing Google\'s December preview. Max tier: Combines open-web search with MCP connectors and private file uploads. Charts and infographics generate natively. Data partners: PitchBook, S&P and FactSet are building MCP servers that pipe premium financial data straight into the output. Private-only mode: Turn off the open web and run the agent only against your internal corpus. Research-heavy work is the softest target AI has in enterprise. Analysts at banks, consultants at strategy firms and junior lawyers all bill hours that mostly involve reading and synthesising documents. Google has now turned that into an API call any developer can wire into a product. The next round of vertical partnerships will tell us which industries move first. Meta is logging employee keystrokes to train AI agents Meta has begun recording screenshots, keystrokes, mouse movements and app activity on US employees\' work laptops, according to internal memos seen by Business Insider. The programme is called the Model Capability Initiative. There is no opt-out. Scope: VSCode, Google Chat, Gmail and Metamate, Meta\'s internal AI assistant. Developers are the heaviest targets. No opt-out: CTO Andrew Bosworth reportedly told staff participation is mandatory. Layoffs in scope: 8,000 staff exit on 20 May. MCI began logging them a month before their end date. Framing: Meta presented the programme as helping its models \"get better simply by doing their daily work.\" Robotics labs have spent years filming humans doing physical tasks to teach systems when to grasp or step. Meta is running the same playbook for software, except the demo subjects are its own staff and many are being captured a month out from losing their jobs. Expect lawsuits first, then union-style agreements about AI training data rights. This is the first enterprise-scale example of knowledge work being turned directly into training data. SpaceX puts a $60 billion option on Cursor SpaceX claims it has secured the rights to acquire Cursor for $60 billion later this year, or to pay $10 billion for ongoing joint work. The two are building SpaceXAI, Musk\'s attempt to catch Codex and Claude Code in agentic coding. Cursor is separately raising a $2 billion round with a16z, Nvidia and Thrive Capital reportedly in the mix. Stated goal: SpaceX framed the arrangement as a path to \"the world\'s best coding and knowledge work AI.\" Cursor\'s trajectory: Valuation has climbed sharply on the strength of its IDE and coding agent. Independence path: The new round gives Cursor enough capital to stay independent if SpaceX does not exercise. xAI reboot: Musk is using SpaceXAI to restart his developer story after losing ground to Codex and Claude Code. Musk\'s pattern is to buy reach when he cannot build it fast enough. He did it with Twitter. He did it with xAI and x.ai. This one would be bigger by an order of magnitude. The optionality matters more than the deal itself. Either Cursor stays independent with a nuclear war chest, or Musk ends up owning the sharpest coding interface on the market. Either way, the top of the coding-AI table gets a third serious player. Tool of the Day: Exa Deep Max Exa released Deep Max today. SOTA agentic search, tops rivals on accuracy while running 20 times faster. Plans queries, runs them in parallel and delivers a synthesised answer with sources. Built for analysts, researchers and anyone buried in tabs. Try it at exa.ai. Paste in a research brief that usually takes you an afternoon and see what comes back. Light Bytes Bitcoin: Killing Satoshi premieres at Cannes: First studio AI feature film. $70M budget against a $300M conventional projection. Genspark launches Build on Opus 4.7: Vibe-coding tool generating apps from prompts. Takes on Lovable, v0 and Replit Agent. Jerry Tworek starts Core Automation: OpenAI\'s former research VP launches a new lab with OpenAI, Anthropic and DeepMind alumni. Meta takes three more from Thinking Machines Lab: Seven founding members of Mira Murati\'s lab now at Meta. Google open-sources DESIGN.md: A portable config from Stitch that teaches AI agents a project\'s colours, accessibility rules and brand system. Deezer hits 75K AI tracks a day: 44% of uploads, only 1 to 3% of streams, 85% flagged as fraudulent.\nKeep reading on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/image-model-thinks-genai-works-ajy9f?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3B4Har7%2BmKR5CsyFgeHj%2B6SQ%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=0oKcZT2wmuUIc1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_-null-icq8ji~moafax34~uc-null-null&eid=icq8ji-moafax34-uc\n  \n      \n\n----------------------------------------\n\nThis email was intended for Yashica Lohar (Flipkart GWC 7.0 Scholar | CSE ’28 @ IIIT Jabalpur | 2 ⭐ @Codechef)\nLearn why we included this: https://www.linkedin.com/help/linkedin/answer/4788?lang=en&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3B4Har7%2BmKR5CsyFgeHj%2B6SQ%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=0oKcZT2wmuUIc1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer-null-icq8ji~moafax34~uc-null-null&eid=icq8ji-moafax34-uc\nYou are receiving LinkedIn notification emails. Others can see that you are a subscriber.\n\n\nUnsubscribe: https://www.linkedin.com/series-notifications/?action=unsubscribe&memberToken=ADoAAEIlwb4B7wSObgzu320aKxhPrfHhU2UtxwY&newsletterId=7075090904906149891&newsletterTitle=The Atlas&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_emai