ck.\n\nFocus on Structuring GitHub Repository Today!\nToday is all about the Quick Run Check: Ensure your PPT highlights all your core\nfeatures, your Demo Video is clear and audible, and your GitHub Repository is\nwell-structured.\n\nClick Here to Submit Your Prototype\n[https://9hdppg0c.r.ap-southeast-2.awstrack.me/L0/https:%2F%2Fhack2skill.com%2Fevent%2Fsolution-challenge-2026%2Fregistration%3Futm_source=hack2skill%26utm_medium=homepage%26utm_campaign=%26utm_term=%26utm_content=/1/0108019dc2ebdef4-2028b64d-335c-47e8-a7d8-3d61fd687589-000000/5L1DjrVhqf9PW5mHSTwOeP_WccE=256]\nNote: It is mandatory to use the given presentation template for project\nsubmission: Download now →\n[https://9hdppg0c.r.ap-southeast-2.awstrack.me/L0/https:%2F%2Fdocs.google.com%2Fpresentation%2Fd%2F1xiWuM0vqqjPUR-eAR4PIzu_CaLucYEJfLZOE8GXYyjo%2Fexport%2Fpptx/1/0108019dc2ebdef4-2028b64d-335c-47e8-a7d8-3d61fd687589-000000/-pKzmRnjvswQ-yKTGc-0w_xngWE=256]\n\n\nHOW YOUR PROTOTYPE WILL BE EVALUATED:\n\n• Your Prototype Deck will be the first thing to be reviewed.\n\n• Only if your PPT passes, your Video Demo will be reviewed.\n\n• Only if your Demo Video passes, your Prototype will be reviewed.\n\n• Only if all the above submission passes, your GitHub Repo will be reviewed.\n\nSubmission Requirements\n1\nProblem Statement\n\nClearly define the issue your team is addressing.\n\n2\nSolution Overview\n\nExplain how your prototype solves the problem.\n\n3\nPrototype Link\n\nA live MVP link demonstrating core functionality.\n\n4\nProject Deck\n\nDetailing your solution, architecture, and impact.\n\n5\nGitHub Repository\n\nPublic link to your well-structured source code.\n\n6\nDemo Video\n\nA short video showcasing your solution in action.\n\n\nYOUR TREASURE MAP TO SUBMISSION\n\nTo support your prototype submission, we’ve created a simple 6-step,\nbeginner-friendly guide with practical prompts to help you get started—even if\nyou’re starting today.\n\nClick Here for the Guide\n[https://9hdppg0c.r.ap-southeast-2.awstrack.me/L0/https:%2F%2Fdocs.google.com%2Fdocument%2Fd%2F1nBtrGqdJWuIOOMAVkw3rfKohoaTMl8UyOYf4QB3qADs%2Fedit%3Ftab=t.0%23heading=h.r62xehm9jalv/1/0108019dc2ebdef4-2028b64d-335c-47e8-a7d8-3d61fd687589-000000/jyJ9vh8nypNqT31LVOc_aqEhU4s=256]\n\nFocus on clearly conveying your idea, not building a fully polished\nsolution—impact matters more than perfection.\n\nReminder: Perfection isn\'t required for a draft submission. If you are still\ntweaking the code, submit your current progress today to stay ahead of the\ndeadline.\n\nRegards,\nTeam Hack2skill\n[https://9hdppg0c.r.ap-southeast-2.awstrack.me/I0/0108019dc2ebdef4-2028b64d-335c-47e8-a7d8-3d61fd687589-000000/jrCky8LtouDKwq8jsAS3wD__KY0=256]\n\n \n\n \n\nFacebook\n[https://h2svision.github.io/publicAssets/vision/h2s_facebook.png]https://9hdppg0c.r.ap-southeast-2.awstrack.me/L0/https:%2F%2Fwww.facebook.com%2Fhack2skill%2F/1/0108019dc2ebdef4-2028b64d-335c-47e8-a7d8-3d61fd687589-000000/AU5G4dxEWaMpEhBrRp6LY7-E3cE=256\nTwitter\n[https://h2svision.github.io/publicAssets/vision/h2s_twitter.png]https://9hdppg0c.r.ap-southeast-2.awstrack.me/L0/https:%2F%2Ftwitter.com%2Fhack2skill/1/0108019dc2ebdef4-2028b64d-335c-47e8-a7d8-3d61fd687589-000000/Jmr_SEASeKc50kGDdUOcTvY00mA=256\nInstagram\n[https://h2svision.github.io/publicAssets/vision/h2s_instagram.png]https://9hdppg0c.r.ap-southeast-2.awstrack.me/L0/https:%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Fhack2skill%2F/1/0108019dc2ebdef4-2028b64d-335c-47e8-a7d8-3d61fd687589-000000/tvZn2ys-3i72z2PuJT-os25GaQY=256\nLinkedIn\n[https://h2svision.github.io/publicAssets/vision/h2s_linkedin.png]https://9hdppg0c.r.ap-southeast-2.awstrack.me/L0/https:%2F%2Fwww.linkedin.com%2Fcompany%2Fhack2skill%2F/1/0108019dc2ebdef4-2028b64d-335c-47e8-a7d8-3d61fd687589-000000/3l-toUDn3svvT4JZCDpuKJtZ_W4=256\nYouTube\n[https://h2svision.github.io/publicAssets/vision/h2s_youtube.png]https://9hdppg0c.r.ap-southeast-2.awstrack.me/L0/https:%2F%2Fwww.youtube.com%2Fchannel%2FUCw2br80x_dpvJoAsSCC-bGA/1/0108019dc2ebdef4-2028b64d-335c-47e8-a7d8-3d61fd687589-000000/h9ow5FA5M6laiElMUOl2c8YAdqI=256\nWhatsApp\n[https://h2svision.github.io/publicAssets/vision/h2s_whatsapp.png]https://9hdppg0c.r.ap-southeast-2.awstrack.me/L0/https:%2F%2Fapi.whatsapp.com%2Fsend%2F%3Fphone=919870330830%26text%26type=phone_number%26app_absent=0/1/0108019dc2ebdef4-2028b64d-335c-47e8-a7d8-3d61fd687589-000000/xUnUTvr7zg8_-aJTQvaJXe2BkeM=256\nDiscord\n[https://h2svision.github.io/publicAssets/vision/h2s_discord.png]https://9hdppg0c.r.ap-southeast-2.awstrack.me/L0/https:%2F%2Fdiscord.com%2Fchannels%2F900275142217830402%2F969491518371008562/1/0108019dc2ebdef4-2028b64d-335c-47e8-a7d8-3d61fd687589-000000/hZhP8BGpxQP2kM_21kBSe0ln7MI=256\n\n© 2026. Built on Hack2skill\n[https://9hdppg0c.r.ap-southeast-2.awstrack.me/L0/https:%2F%2Fhack2skill.com%2F/1/0108019dc2ebdef4-2028b64d-335c-47e8-a7d8-3d61fd687589-000000/eZT6Th421MlmLeUPCYcrX_ClVoQ=256]','incoming-complaint','INCOMING',NULL,'2026-06-21 16:52:16',NULL,NULL,NULL),('a634acc7-6633-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7','d69e6b3d-662d-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Dear Solution Challenge 2026 - Build with AI,\n\nWe have received your ticket regarding: \"4 Days Left: Check your GitHub Repo\".\n\nYour reference ID is #d69e6b3d. This issue has been logged and is currently under review by our team.','approved-draft-placeholder','INCOMING',NULL,'2026-06-21 16:52:16',NULL,NULL,NULL),('a639e6de-6d97-11f1-89d5-e86538d58b3c','e754e5ff-6703-11f1-a09a-8107a00d61c0','This may surprise you: SpaceX is buying Cursor for $60 billion','newsletters-noreply@linkedin.com','loharyashica@gmail.com',NULL,'The Atlas\n\nBy GenAI Works\n\n\nBy GenAI Works\n\n    \n    \n    Read this article on LinkedIn to join the conversation\nRead on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/may-surprise-you-spacex-buying-cursor-60-billion-genai-works-spuue?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3Bw09c6yhvSUKUOczZFEIEZA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=3gLLWE62AX7sk1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_-null-icq8ji~mqicjmsw~pa-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mqicjmsw-pa\n  \nWelcome back! Pause for a second. SpaceX\'s reported $60 billion Cursor deal shows that owning the developer workspace may now matter more than building another model. A new CVPR paper shows that better image segmentation was already hidden inside frozen vision models. The trick was using the features differently. Databricks launched Omnigent to help different AI agents work together instead of running separately. And Microsoft made Copilot Cowork generally available with usage-based pricing, turning agent work into something companies can track, consume, and meter like cloud computing. In today\'s Atlas Newsletter: Why SpaceX paid $60 billion for Cursor: the AI war stopped being about the biggest model and became a land grab for where work happens. Segmentation with zero training and one example: a CVPR paper pulls a hidden bias out of frozen features and the accuracy was waiting there all along. Your agents do not talk to each other: Databricks open-sourced Omnigent, a meta-harness that sits above Claude Code, Codex and Pi to compose, control and share them. Microsoft just made agent work a metered utility: Copilot Cowork hit general availability with usage-based pricing, Microsoft\'s first major pricing shift in nearly two decades. PS: Want to collaborate with the biggest GTM engine and distribution moat for AI companies? You can feature your tool with us and reach millions. Why did SpaceX buy Cursor for $60 billion? Ever notice this pattern? The biggest AI moves lately are not about models. They are about real estate. SpaceX just agreed to buy Anysphere, the company behind Cursor, in an all-stock deal worth about $60 billion.  The merger was signed June 16, days after SpaceX\'s blockbuster IPO.  A rocket and satellite company buying a coding assistant sounds strange until you see what they actually bought. They did not buy a model. xAI already has Grok. They bought the place developers sit all day. Cursor\'s revenue tells the story. It hit a $4 billion annualized run rate, more than double where it sat in February.  That growth is not coming from autocomplete. It is coming from owning the surface where engineers decide what to build, what to refactor, what to test and what to ship. Here is the pattern once you start looking for it. Anthropic, OpenAI and Google have spent two years fighting over the same layer. Claude Code, Codex and Cursor keep rewriting how software gets shipped. The model is becoming a commodity. The interface to real work is becoming the prize. SpaceX had a choice spelled out in the original option: pay roughly $10 billion to keep collaborating, or buy the whole thing for $60 billion.  They bought the whole thing. When a company pays a 6x premium to own the interface instead of renting it, that is the pattern showing its hand. Own the model and you own a capability. Own the place where work happens and you own the work. Software development is just the first battlefield. The same pattern is coming for design, legal, finance and support. Watch where the surfaces get bought next. The next AI war is a land grab for where work happens. The biggest model is no longer the thing worth owning. By Sunday night, you\'ll have Claude running your desktop. Anthropic ships new Claude capabilities almost every week. Skills, Connectors, Cowork, vibe coding. Most professionals are still using Claude for one-off chats, missing the modes that actually change how work gets done. Outskill\'s 2-Day Claude AI Mastery Workshop is a live, end-to-end walkthrough taught by mentors who condensed 800+ hours of research and real-world testing into one weekend. You\'ll walk away knowing: How to use Claude\'s three modes: Chat, Cowork, and Code How to set up Skills and Connectors that automate your Notion, files, and desktop How to vibe code working apps and dashboards alongside 10+ tools that pair with Claude Saturday and Sunday, 10 AM to 7 PM EST. Free for the next 48 hours. Save your free spot Learn AI from the people who use it every day AI is rewriting how work gets done, and the gap between people who keep up and people who fall behind is widening fast.  GenAI Academy is how you stay on the right side of it.  Every course is built and curated by working AI experts, not theorists, so you walk away with moves you can use the same afternoon. Here is what to start with. Claude Starter Course (free) The fastest way from \"I opened Claude once\" to using it well every day. Set up, prompt, and get real output in an hour. Claude Practitioner\'s Guide to AI Agents (free) Go past chat. Build agents that take a task and carry it through, with the patterns practitioners actually rely on. How to Stop Hitting Your Claude Limit (free) The limit usually hits mid-task on something small. This course shows you why, then hands you 24 fixes across Claude.ai, Cowork and Claude Code so it stops happening. Mastering Claude Code (early bird offer) The deep track. Advanced prompting, real projects, and the habits that separate casual users from power users. Locked-in pricing for early sign-ups. Claude Excel for Finance (early bird) Turn Claude into a finance analyst that lives in your spreadsheets. Modeling, cleanup and analysis at a pace manual work cannot match. Early-bird pricing while it lasts. Three free courses to start. Two early-bird tracks when you want to go further.  Pick one and put it to work this week. Image segmentation work with zero training and a single example This may surprise you. Researchers made image segmentation work with zero training and a single example. Segmenting a new object usually means labeled masks or a fine-tuned model. Both fall apart the moment you switch domains. A new CVPR paper says one example is enough. INSID3 segments anything from a single annotated image. Zero training. Everything runs on frozen DINOv3 features. No decoder. No mask supervision. No SAM. The clever part is what the team found hiding in those features. The patches carried a positional bias. They were matching by location instead of meaning. Two patches looked similar to the model because they sat in the same spot, not because they showed the same thing. The fix is three steps: Feed pure noise through the encoder Pull out the bias direction with SVD Subtract it from the features Clean features then match the example to the target, and clustering builds the final mask from the coherent regions. The payoff: accuracy jumps 7.5% mIoU over the previous best, with 3x fewer parameters, running faster. The same approach handles medical, underwater and aerial images without retraining. Here is the part worth holding onto.  The capability was already sitting inside a frozen model. Nobody trained anything new.  They found a bias, removed it, and the performance was there waiting. The win came from using the model better, not building a bigger one. That lesson travels well past segmentation. Most people leaving capability on the table are not running a weaker model, they are running one carrying baggage that gets in its own way. What else is hiding inside self-supervised features? Nobody mentions this part of the agent boom: the agents do not work together Omnigent is the open source meta-harness that combines, controls and shares your agents. It lets you build multi-agent coding and custom agents, sitting above Claude Code, Codex, Pi and agent SDKs so you can compose them. It also adds live collaboration and rich control policies. Omnigent came out of what Databricks and Neon saw in practice.  Engineers were combining multiple agents into loops, and that was hard to do above the harness layer. The fix was a uniform API above any harness that makes richer features possible on top. For composition, you create multi-agent teams across different harnesses, or swap harness and model mid-session and mid-loop. For collaboration, Omnigent puts a common UI above any agent, with native app, web and mobile, plus live collaboration with teammates. No copy-pasting text and screenshots between agents, Slack and Docs. For security and cost control, Omnigent adds a contextual policy model that tracks session state. It can ask for permission to git push after an agent downloads an untrusted npm package, or pause an agent after every $100 of spend. A strong OS sandbox from the security team backs it. A lot of work is about to move past the individual agent and up to a meta-harness layer like this as agents get more widely adopted.  That is why Databricks is open sourcing this foundation, built from real coding and custom agent needs at Databricks and Neon. The project now handles full coding and product management with a single interface to every agent.  It is open source under Apache 2.0.  Try it and join the community to build it with others. Pause for a second. Microsoft turned agents into a metered utility Copilot Cowork went generally available on June 16, and the pricing change underneath it is the part worth reading twice.  It is Microsoft\'s first major pricing model shift in nearly two decades. After a three-month Frontier preview, more than half of the Fortune 500 are already using Copilot Cowork, along with Accenture, Avanade, Advance Local, Capital Group, Koch, LTM, Ooredoo Qatar and Zurich Insurance. What it does: you define a complex, long-running, multi-tool task and Cowork runs it end to end, returning a finished result instead of a draft.  Microsoft points to real examples.  One engineering team taught it to safely edit batch-job spreadsheets and generate dependency flow charts after every change.  One team compared nearly 4,000 files across two product versions.  One sales lead aimed it at a stalled pipeline and got back a ranked list of at-risk opportunities with the exact follow-up that had gone cold on each, turning a week of review into one morning. Five things Microsoft says set it apart: cloud hosting so tasks keep running with your laptop off, native Work IQ grounding in your business systems, security inside the Microsoft 365 trust boundary, multi-model design, and usage-based billing that charges only for what runs.  Microsoft\'s own testing claims Copilot Cowork averages 30 to 40% cheaper per prompt than Claude Cowork using the Microsoft 365 connector.  That number is Microsoft\'s, on Microsoft\'s tests, so weigh it accordingly. The model layer is the tell. Copilot Cowork runs multi-model, today leaning on Anthropic\'s Opus 4.8 and Sonnet 4.6, with a fine-tuned in-house model called Cowork 1 due in the coming weeks to push task cost down further. You pick the model the task needs rather than getting locked to one. On pricing: Cowork requires the Microsoft 365 Copilot license, then bills per task in Copilot Credits across four inputs, model use, context retrieval, tool calls and runtime.  Predictable seat fee for the everyday Copilot surface, metered usage for the heavy agentic work. The shift to watch is metered agent work landing inside the enterprise suite most companies already pay for.  When the agent layer becomes a utility you bill by the task, the question stops being which assistant to buy and becomes how much autonomous work you are willing to run. Everything else you shouldn’t miss: Lovable\'s new toolbar: Lovable shipped a four-mode toolbar that lets you annotate, select elements, edit text inline and drop comments onto your project, with inline text edits now writing back to your database automatically. Android 17 goes agent-native: Android 17 added AppFunctions and Android MCP so apps can expose tools that on-device agents discover and run, Google\'s first real step toward an \"intelligence system\" baked across the platform. Qwen talks to robots: Qwen-RobotWorld is a language-conditioned video world model that uses plain text as a single action interface across robotics, navigation and driving. Codex gets a browser: OpenAI gave Codex Chrome DevTools Protocol access so it can profile JavaScript and edit live sites in real time, opt-in for now and blocked in the EEA, UK and Switzerland. Learn more about AI from the experts building it 📸 Follow us on Instagram for fast, visual AI updates in 30 seconds.  🐝 Subscribe to our Generative AI daily newsletter — trusted by 120 000+ subscribers — to stay ahead of the latest AI news across tech, education, and business.  📺 Watch us on YouTube to hear insights directly from leading AI voices, builders, and innovators. 🐦 Follow us on X for breaking AI news and real-time industry updates. 🧠 Learn how to build your next AI application with practical resources and expert guidance. 🚀 Explore investment opportunities in the future of AI and join our community-backed growth journey. PS: Want to collaborate with the biggest GTM engine and distribution moat for AI companies? You can feature your tool with us and reach millions.\nKeep reading on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/may-surprise-you-spacex-buying-cursor-60-billion-genai-works-spuue?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3Bw09c6yhvSUKUOczZFEIEZA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=3gLLWE62AX7sk1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_-null-icq8ji~mqicjmsw~pa-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mqicjmsw-pa\n  \n      \n\n----------------------------------------\n\nThis email was intended for Yashica Lohar (Flipkart GWC 7.0 Scholar | CSE ’28 @ IIIT Jabalpur | 2 ⭐ @Codechef)\nLearn why we included this: https://www.linkedin.com/help/linkedin/answer/4788?lang=en&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3Bw09c6yhvSUKUOczZFEIEZA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=3gLLWE62AX7sk1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer&trkEmail=em