-21 16:52:16',NULL,NULL,NULL),('a764c168-6633-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7','d69e6b3d-662d-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'The Atlas\n\nBy GenAI Works\n\n\nBy GenAI Works\n\n    \n    \n    Read this article on LinkedIn to join the conversation\nRead on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/microsoft-cant-keep-up-ai-demand-anymore-genai-works-ldmlf?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3B5DBSBrWARmq7QEYhJzdOqA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=3BtXSqdX905sg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_-null-icq8ji~mona90bq~lo-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mona90bq-lo\n  \nWelcome to our special Friday financial newsletter! Microsoft\'s quarterly earnings contained a line that matters more than any of the headline numbers. The company is supply-constrained through all of 2026. Alphabet extended its AI capex commitment into 2027 in the same earnings window. Goldman Sachs led a healthcare-AI round that has an IPO desk signature all over it. And VCs have put $18.8 billion into AI startups that have not shipped a single product. Here is everything you need to know today. In today’s Generative AI Newsletter: Microsoft Q3: The supply constraint admission changes the bull case for AI infrastructure stocks. Alphabet Q1: The 2027 capex flag is more important than the 2026 revision. Aidoc Series E: Goldman Sachs just placed an 18-month IPO bet on hospital AI. Big Tech exodus: $18.8 billion has gone into 2025-vintage AI labs whose founders have not shipped anything yet. Microsoft can\'t build fast enough to meet AI demand Microsoft reported Q3 earnings on April 29 with revenue of $82.89B and AI annualized revenue of $37B, up 123% year over year. The company now has over 20 million paid Microsoft 365 Copilot seats. Its 2026 calendar-year capex guide sits at $190B, including $25B attributable to higher component pricing, with Q4 spend expected to exceed $40B. Management said supply is constrained through all of 2026. The details: AI ARR: At $37B, Microsoft\'s AI revenue line is now competitive with OpenAI\'s $25B run rate and Anthropic\'s $30-40B run rate. Supply constraint: Management explicitly confirmed that capacity to deploy AI is the limiting factor through year-end, not customer demand. That framing removes the \"is AI capex excessive\" thesis from the conversation. Higher component pricing: $25B of the $190B guide reflects elevated component costs, meaning the supply chain is inflating faster than original models assumed. Q4 capex over $40B: That continues the acceleration from $37.5B in Q3 and represents Microsoft\'s steepest quarterly AI buildout yet. Microsoft\'s report show that the demand for AI is there, and they can do better work, but the physical buildout is the main constraint. Fiber, power and cooling companies sit closer to that constraint than chipmakers do, which is where the next leg of the trade plays out. Alphabet extended the AI infrastructure cycle into 2027 Alphabet entered this earnings week under the same pressure as every hyperscaler. Investors wanted to know whether 2026 marks the ceiling for AI infrastructure spending. The answer came not from the 2026 capex line but from CFO Anat Ashkenazi, who said 2027 spend will be \"significantly increased.\" Google Cloud grew 63% to $20B for the quarter, capex came in at $190B for 2026, up $5B from prior guidance and shares popped 7% after hours toward a $4.3T market cap. The details: Prior consensus: The prevailing question this week was whether 2026 marks peak hyperscaler capex. Alphabet\'s CFO settled it. Cloud at 63% growth: Growth at this scale almost always decelerates. Google Cloud accelerating at $20B quarterly revenue tells you AI workloads are the driver, not legacy migrations finishing up. $4.3T market cap: The stock move validated the capex commitment. That is a regime change from 12 months ago, when capex raises were read as risk. Chip stock implications: Nvidia, Broadcom and AMD forward estimates have been built assuming 2026 as the peak year. The 2027 commitment from Alphabet extends the visible demand runway by 12 months. The 2027 commitment resets the forward model for an entire supply chain. Chip fabs, fiber manufacturers, power utilities and data center construction firms plan capacity years in advance, and Alphabet\'s forward commitment triggers their own buildout extensions. The AI infrastructure cycle just got longer in ways that affect employment, energy demand and industrial investment well beyond what Nvidia\'s share price captures. What the market is telling us Microsoft and Alphabet both disclosed $190B in 2026 AI capex this week. Meta revised to $125B-$145B. Amazon is tracking $200B. Combined, that is roughly $700B in AI infrastructure spend from four companies this calendar year, up about 77% on last year\'s record. The market did not treat them the same way. Public markets: The stocks rewarded Alphabet and Microsoft for raising guidance because both companies can point to a specific AI revenue number. The market has moved from pricing AI spend on faith to pricing it on evidence. Supply chain: Optical fiber, grid-connected power and liquid cooling are where the next phase of the trade plays out. Corning disclosed two new hyperscale fiber deals this week described as \"similar size and scope to the Meta agreement,\" meaning billions committed 24-36 months out. Meta: Meta posted 33% revenue growth and still dropped 7% after hours. Its AI return is embedded inside advertising efficiency rather than a visible new line item and investors won\'t price what they can\'t see. Two years of investor patience on AI infrastructure has compressed into a single question about revenue conversion. Alphabet and Microsoft answered it this week. Meta has roughly four quarters to produce a visible AI revenue line before investors reprice the gap from temporary to structural. At $145B in annual capex, a structural repricing of Meta would be the first real crack in the mega-cap tech trade since the AI cycle began. Goldman Sachs just placed an 18-month IPO bet on hospital AI Goldman Sachs does not lead a late-stage healthcare round without an IPO desk relationship in play. When the bank stepped up to lead Aidoc\'s latest round, it was pricing a path to public markets, not placing a growth bet. The round closed at $150M, with Nvidia NVentures, General Catalyst and SoftBank Investment Advisors alongside Goldman, bringing total outside funding above $500M. Aidoc\'s software reads medical imaging studies, flags critical findings in real time and routes them to the right clinician before the radiologist reaches the worklist. The details: Goldman leading: Goldman growth equity does not lead a Series E without the IPO desk involved. Nvidia NVentures participating alongside a bulge-bracket bank confirms this is priced as a pre-IPO capital event. Why healthcare AI now: The FDA has cleared over 700 AI-enabled medical devices. The regulatory risk that suppressed clinical-AI valuations in 2023 and 2024 has substantially lifted. The comp problem: There is no publicly traded clinical-AI company at Aidoc\'s scale. An Aidoc IPO creates the first listed benchmark for the entire category, which is why the round matters beyond the $150M itself. $500M total raised: That figure indicates Aidoc has been built as a public-market asset from early on. The revenue base to support a listing is there. An Aidoc IPO matters well beyond the company itself. Without a publicly traded clinical-AI benchmark, hospital procurement conversations happen in a pricing vacuum, with contracts negotiated on bespoke terms that slow adoption across thousands of health systems. A listed Aidoc gives every hospital CFO a market price for this category of software, which compresses procurement cycles. The capital markets outcome here has a direct line to how quickly AI-assisted diagnosis reaches patients at scale. VCs put $18.8 billion into AI labs that have not shipped anything The most unusual thing about the current AI funding cycle is that the largest checks are going to teams without a product. Capital has fully decoupled from delivery timelines. CNBC reported on April 28 that venture investors have deployed $18.8B into AI startups founded since the start of 2025, a cohort on pace to surpass the $27.9B raised by 2024-vintage AI labs across their entire history. Mira Murati\'s Thinking Machines Lab, David Silver\'s Ineffable Intelligence and Yann LeCun\'s AMI Labs have all priced at multi-billion valuations before delivering a product. The details: What is being priced: Founder pedigree. None of these labs have public benchmarks, released products or disclosed revenue. The thesis is that researchers who built the most capable models at OpenAI, DeepMind and Meta will do it again independently. $18.8B in pre-product labs: That compares to the $27.9B raised by the 2024-vintage cohort, which at least had product timelines in place. Capital is now priced on team and ambition. The compounding pressure: Most of these rounds carry 18 to 24-month deployment timelines. By mid-2027, LPs will demand revenue evidence. A single large failure here re-marks the entire 2025 vintage simultaneously. Departure rationale: Researchers cite autonomy and upside. They are leaving winning environments, which means Big Tech is inadvertently subsidizing the training pipelines of its own competitors. The downstream consequences are already visible in the talent market. When pedigree alone commands multi-billion valuations, compensation expectations inside every established AI company reprice upward, forcing Google, Microsoft and Meta into spending wars they did not budget for. The stakes extend well beyond a few VC funds. A category-wide failure to convert by mid-2027 would tighten early-stage AI funding for years and reshape which companies can still attract the talent that started this cycle. Top AI deals of the week Rogo, Series D, $160M: AI agents for investment banking, with JPMorgan Growth Equity Partners investing alongside Kleiner Perkins, Sequoia and Thrive. JPMorgan on both the strategic and investor side of the same deal means the bank has settled build-versus-buy. Sereact, Series B, $110M: German robotics software company building a world model for factory robots, expanding into the US. Headline VC led. Firestorm Labs, Series B, $82M: Deployable drone factories with Lockheed Martin and In-Q-Tel on the cap table. That combination almost always precedes a program-of-record award. Manifest OS, Series A, $60M at $750M: Largest Series A in legal tech history. Legal AI had been discounted on the assumption that bar regulation would slow adoption. This mark removes that discount. General Analysis, seed, $10M: Agent security infrastructure. Founded by researchers out of Nvidia, Cohere and DeepMind. The round is small. The founding team is the tell.\nKeep reading on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/microsoft-cant-keep-up-ai-demand-anymore-genai-works-ldmlf?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3B5DBSBrWARmq7QEYhJzdOqA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=3BtXSqdX905sg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_-null-icq8ji~mona90bq~lo-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mona90bq-lo\n  \n      \n\n----------------------------------------\n\nThis email was intended for Yashica Lohar (Flipkart GWC 7.0 Scholar | CSE ’28 @ IIIT Jabalpur | 2 ⭐ @Codechef)\nLearn why we included this: https://www.linkedin.com/help/linkedin/answer/4788?lang=en&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3B5DBSBrWARmq7QEYhJzdOqA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=3BtXSqdX905sg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follo