%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=0hsYImzm-lfcg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_-null-icq8ji~moxa0neq~yc-null-null&eid=icq8ji-moxa0neq-yc\n  \nWelcome back to Financial Friday! The Pentagon picked eight AI vendors for classified work and Anthropic was not on the list. The Financial Times pulled balance sheets on the four hyperscalers and the headline number is brutal. $725B in AI infrastructure spending this year, dragging combined free cash flow to a decade low. Cloudflare went the other way, cutting 1,100 jobs to \"fundamentally\" embrace AI agents, and the stock dropped 18% the same day. And in the most disorienting compute stat of the week, Anthropic added 13.8 GW of new commitments over thirty days while OpenAI added zero. In today\'s Newsletter: The Pentagon picks its AI vendors. Anthropic isn\'t one. Big Tech\'s $725B AI binge crushes free cash flow. Cloudflare bleeds while prediction markets bets on more incidents. NVIDIA still wins the AI stock fight for 2026. Key market indicators. The Pentagon picked eight AI vendors. Anthropic was not invited The Pentagon signed deals with eight companies for AI use on classified networks: OpenAI, Google, Microsoft, Amazon, NVIDIA, Oracle, Reflection AI and SpaceX (which now owns xAI).  Anthropic, the lab that has spent more public effort than any peer on AI safety, was deliberately excluded. The Trump administration severed ties earlier this year after Anthropic refused to allow Claude to be used for autonomous weapons or mass surveillance under the standard \"all lawful purposes\" clause. Anthropic sued. A federal judge in California blocked the government\'s effort last month and Anthropic still did not make this cohort. The procurement architecture is the story. By signing eight vendors, the Pentagon removes any one lab\'s leverage to set ethical terms on military AI. If Anthropic won\'t play, replace Anthropic.  That works because at this layer of capability the technology has no moat. Eight other vendors will deliver a good-enough Claude alternative and the Trump administration is making a deliberate decision that ethics in AI procurement is a vendor problem, not a constitutional one. For investors, the names matter. Defense AI revenue is durable, contractually committed and largely insulated from the AI hype cycle. NVIDIA, Microsoft, Google, Amazon and Oracle now have it. Palantir already did. SpaceX got xAI in through the parent company side door, which is the kind of corporate structuring move worth remembering when the next AI exclusion list comes out. Big Tech\'s $725B AI spree just crushed free cash flow to a decade low The Financial Times pulled the numbers and they are ugly. The four hyperscalers (Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta) are on track to spend roughly $725B on AI infrastructure this year. Combined quarterly free cash flow is projected to fall to about $4B, down from $45B per quarter since 2020. That is a decade low. The details: Amazon: Spending around $200B in 2026 and may burn $10B in cash. Andy Jassy compares it to early AWS investment. Meta: Paused buybacks, issued tens of billions in debt and cut staff to fund AI buildout. Alphabet: Suspended buybacks for the first time since 2015 to free up cash for compute. Microsoft: Servers, networking and software assets on the balance sheet have more than tripled since 2022. The asset-light era for Big Tech is over. These companies are now infrastructure-heavy industrials, closer to utilities than software platforms. Off-balance-sheet financing through SPVs is hiding part of the cost and accounting professors flat-out say the actual cash flows are worse than reported.  The historical comparison The Financial Times article keeps reaching for is the telecom fiber boom, which ended in massive overcapacity and a decade of low returns. Worth holding that in mind every time someone calls this spend \"rational.\" Polymarket is already pricing in the next Cloudflare outage Matthew Prince, Cloudflare’s CEO, told investors on Wednesday\'s earnings call that they’re going \"agentic AI-first,\" cutting 1,100 jobs (about 20% of headcount) because internal AI usage has grown 600% in three months and \"fundamentally changed\" how the company operates.  Ironically, five months ago on December 31, Prince was on X celebrating Cloudflare\'s recruiting team for hiring 2,042 new people through 2025 with a 91.9% offer acceptance rate. \"Companies are just collections of people,\" he wrote, \"so recruiting well is critical.\" This week he laid off 1,100 of them. The stock fell 18% the same day, per CNBC. Severance is $140M to $150M for 2026, with departing employees keeping the equivalent of their full base pay through year-end. The package itself is generous. The framing is what the market choked on.  Telling Wall Street that agents replaced your engineers is a story analysts have heard from dozens of companies in the last six months. The 18% drop suggests the patience for that pitch is thinning. Two things make the timing read worse than a normal restructuring.  Cloudflare has had six notable outages in the past year, including:  The November 18 global outage that took down X, OpenAI and Anthropic.  The December 5 incident that hit 28% of HTTP traffic.  The February 20 BGP outage that ran more than six hours.  On top of that, Cloudflare Developers spent this same week disclosing security vulnerabilities in React Server Components and Next.js, the kind of distraction a thinned engineering org tends to produce more of.  And on Polymarket, a small ($15K notional) market on whether Cloudflare suffers another critical outage by September 30 is sitting at 78%, with the June 30 cutoff at 38%.  The traders are not predicting catastrophe. They are pricing in that an infrastructure company that publicly told the world it is replacing humans with agents will have a humbling moment within four months. There is a version of this story where Prince is right and the agents do the work.  There is a more honest version where every infrastructure CEO with a slipping stock will reach for the same line, because \"AI is doing the work of the people we just laid off\" is what the market wants to hear, at least until the next outage. Cloudflare gave that pitch its first real-world test and the 18% drop is the early result. NVIDIA vs Palantir: The AI stock fight has a clear winner for 2026 The AI stock question for 2026 keeps coming back to the same matchup. Picks-and-shovels or the application layer. Yahoo Finance came down hard on NVIDIA and the math is hard to argue with. The details: Valuation gap: Palantir trades at 175x forward earnings. NVIDIA trades at 23x. Demand math: Data center spending is projected at $3T to $4T per year by 2030, almost all of it flowing through NVIDIA chips. Government risk: Palantir\'s revenue is heavily tied to federal contracts, exposing it to defense budget cuts. Momentum: NVIDIA recovered in Q2. Palantir is flat with investors hesitant to come back. What it means: Palantir\'s AI Platform is real and the growth is real. But at 175x forward earnings, every quarter has to be perfect. NVIDIA at 23x is priced like a normal company and sits at the bottom of every AI stack on the planet. The boring trade is still the right one. If the $725B Big Tech is spending on AI infrastructure shows up anywhere in earnings, it shows up in NVIDIA\'s first. Market indicators you can\'t miss Anthropic added 13.8 GW of compute commitments in 30 days. OpenAI added zero. Experts are pulling the cumulative compute commitments side by side this week and the chart broke the internet. Between April 7 and May 6, Anthropic announced four major deals totalling 13.8 GW. Across the same window, OpenAI announced no new capacity. Anthropic\'s run, in order: AWS expansion: $100B for 5 GW (April 7). Google + Broadcom TPU deal: 3.5 GW (April 20). Google Cloud commitment: $40B for 5 GW (April 24). SpaceX Colossus 1 in Memphis: 300+ MW with 220K Nvidia GPUs. OpenAI\'s number is zero only for new commitments. It already has 18 GW under contract across Stargate ($500B / 10 GW), AMD MI450 (6 GW) and AWS Trainium2 (2 GW, Feb 27).  What it doesn\'t have is anything announced from late February through May. Most of OpenAI\'s existing capacity rolls out 2026 to 2030. Anthropic\'s SpaceX piece deploys this month. Keep in mind that paper commitments are not delivered capacity. Anthropic has signed for enormous compute and how much actually shows up in the next two quarters is uncertain. The 300 MW from SpaceX is the only piece of the Anthropic run with a hard near-term delivery date. Everything else stretches across the rest of the decade. What has actually changed is the narrative, and that has compounding consequences. Anthropic doubled Claude Code five-hour limits the same hour the SpaceX deal closed, which means it is converting paper commitments into user-facing capacity faster than anyone modelled.  Big Tech\'s $725B is the supply side of this entire newsletter. The question is who turns it into product the fastest. The honest answer right now is Anthropic. Light Bytes Codex jumps into Chrome: OpenAI\'s coding agent now runs across signed-in tabs on Mac and Windows, joining Claude on the \"agents in the browser\" trend. Claude lands across Microsoft 365: Excel, PowerPoint and Word are now generally available with Outlook in beta, two months ahead of Microsoft\'s own Copilot Cowork rollout. Apple\'s camera AirPods reach late-stage testing: Bloomberg reports earbuds with built-in cameras to feed Siri visual context have entered design validation. Anthropic open-sources Petri: The internal alignment auditing tool is now free on GitHub for any lab to test misaligned model behaviors. EU bans nudifier apps, delays everything else: Brussels finalized a ban on AI tools that generate sexualized deepfakes and pushed high-risk AI Act provisions back to December 2027 and August 2028.\nKeep reading on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/pentagon-mad-upset-anthropic-genai-works-hg64e?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3B9eeBvNw%2FRH6Evjw3v9kMMg%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=0hsYImzm-lfcg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_-null-icq8ji~moxa0neq~yc-null-null&eid=icq8ji-moxa0neq-yc\n  \n      \n\n----------------------------------------\n\nThis email was intended for Yashica Lohar (Flipkart GWC 7.0 Scholar | CSE ’28 @ IIIT Jabalpur | 2 ⭐ @Codechef)\nLearn why we included this: https://www.linkedin.com/help/linkedin/answer/4788?lang=en&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3B9eeBvNw%2FRH6Evjw3v9kMMg%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=0hsYImzm-lfcg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer-null-icq8ji~moxa0neq~yc-null-null&eid=icq8ji-moxa0neq-yc\nYou are receiving LinkedIn notification emails. Others can see that you are a subscriber.\n\n\nUnsubscribe: https://www.linkedin.com/series-notifications/?action=unsubscribe&memberToken=ADoAAEIlwb4B7wSObgzu320aKxhPrfHhU2UtxwY&newsletterId=7075090904906149891&newsletterTitle=The Atlas&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3B9eeBvNw%2FRH6Evjw3v9kMMg%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=0hsYImzm-lfcg1&ek=email_series_follow_newsletter_02&e=icq8ji-moxa0neq-yc&eid=icq8ji-moxa0neq-yc&m=unsubscribe&ts=footerGlimmer&li=0&t=plh    ·  Help: https://www.linkedin.com/help/linkedin/answer/67?lang=en&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3B9eeBvNw%2FRH6Evjw3v9kMMg%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=0hsYImzm-lfcg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-help-0-textfooterglimmer&trkEmail=eml-emai