 ID is #d69e6b3d. This issue has been logged and is currently under review by our team.','approved-draft-placeholder','INCOMING',NULL,'2026-06-21 16:52:16',NULL,NULL,NULL),('a8b99c5d-6633-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7','d69e6b3d-662d-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Do More With AI\n\nBy Salesforce\n\n\nBy Salesforce\n\n    \n    \n    Read this article on LinkedIn to join the conversation\nRead on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/its-trust-bust-salesforce-ap1gc?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BLa35hQGBSm%2BosrWH7fwnJA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=1D_VysfWqPkIg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_-null-icq8ji~mp2wegf8~82-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mp2wegf8-82\n  \nYou trust your accountant because they haven\'t lost your money, your doctor because they got rid of that weird red thing, and your best friend because they told you to dump that loser when everyone else kept their mouths shut. Trust isn\'t a vibe. It\'s a track record. Right now, a lot of companies are deploying AI agents and just hoping they don’t go off-script.  This issue is about building agents that earn trust with consistent character, good communication skills, and policies that protect your people and your business. Why? Because prompting an agent to be trustworthy isn\'t the same thing as building one that is. Quite the character In the race to make AI agents smarter, it seems like many companies haven’t stopped to make them trustworthy. If an agent feels inconsistent, goes off-brand, or loses the thread mid-conversation, it quietly erodes trust in your brand. Learn what character-driven AI looks like in practice and why getting it right is less about personality and more about consistently behaving in a way that reflects your company values. Sorry not sorry Knowing when to apologize is a skill. So is knowing when not to. A good agent understands tone, empathy, and that sometimes airing your regrets in public makes everyone uncomfortable. These 10 conversation design principles come straight from our own lessons building Salesforce\'s website support agent. They\'re practical, specific, and useful whether you\'re building your first agent or fine-tuning one that\'s already live. Be careful what you wish for AI agents are like genies that take things very literally. Ask an agent to “clear your afternoon” and it might delete every calendar event, including your daughter\'s 4 p.m. recital. Sure, your afternoon’s open, but you may need to spend the evening buying a pony. The agent did its job as directed, without the necessary guardrails. Here’s what a real agentic governance strategy looks like and why bolting it on after the fact isn\'t an option. This newsletter was curated by Benny Spiewak, Senior Marketing Lead at Salesforce.\nKeep reading on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/its-trust-bust-salesforce-ap1gc?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BLa35hQGBSm%2BosrWH7fwnJA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=1D_VysfWqPkIg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_-null-icq8ji~mp2wegf8~82-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mp2wegf8-82\n  \n      \n\n----------------------------------------\n\nThis email was intended for Yashica Lohar (Flipkart GWC 7.0 Scholar | CSE ’28 @ IIIT Jabalpur | 2 ⭐ @Codechef)\nLearn why we included this: https://www.linkedin.com/help/linkedin/answer/4788?lang=en&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BLa35hQGBSm%2BosrWH7fwnJA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=1D_VysfWqPkIg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer-null-icq8ji~mp2wegf8~82-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mp2weg