oogle services, and what Google expects from you.\n\n• Our Privacy Policy<https://c.gle/AOExmq08NQTx61SPX4KA-vvhzWkCgSJDQYPMBjC8rjxqlbWo_MPoz_kwRgHZM_xHvm4CL-Dog0X4Z85e0m_40_VoGXBvkvzSWBse1Q0qMvKvIp_VOiMowBVDe1tnYaGpj8mfpNmfC4rYFqL-aSpufSCqO8r5ZMs> explains how your information is handled. We work hard to protect your information and put you in control.\n\n• Our Maps User-Generated Content Policy<https://c.gle/AOExmq0RqRzSCfybJNg1CczbMZbOOlc1dGreDFnYoSQ6VL8h0rAE3h2hVjYhQIs6l8A8_PQOHyF734EjWzSFJGhvjaou_uBFt-SQ5ZHSby_olc6IwgDHfW-nF-ce6BGD206RpQduDe12FyoT09v7i1o7ErnaIQf-stx8AsjW0JCRyLD0xlRnMoA8V4X1yqYIcoX8Dq24qopDcMqXfRIsujhFXGJzSc8jhJcS1-hY54eS-tJi4EJlMfukNMnGcrK41ESYdeOYOe4eA0Z_NuOI8oZT3uOl5mhTMplv> is designed to keep content on Google Maps trustworthy and reliable.\n\nNot following these terms and policies may result in removal of your content or restrictions on your account. Your use of the platform is also subject to the Indian Information Technology Rules of 2021 (as amended) (ITR). We are required to remind users that violations of the ITR may have legal consequences. Additionally, we may be required to report certain illegal content, synthetically generated or otherwise, to the appropriate authorities. This mandatory reporting includes content that violates the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 or the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences Act, 2012.\n\nHere are a few tips for contributing to Maps:\n\n• Post genuine reviews: Reviews posted in exchange for a payment, deal or discount are prohibited.\n\n• Share helpful media: Avoid heavily edited photos and selfies, which are less helpful for people trying to decide where to go.\n\n• Report inappropriate content: If you find content<https://c.gle/AOExmq3wiGsvFflJauQdwMzgJ-_ZQLT0AW-H-2AQOnJIc6NEG7eMBQ5ZbycIP6I2bXd3RLeRSkoeN2CRXDoUQd_pd-tEA5vEgSkIN_4TV9bqzfxiilrtZ2aKhpi4EG8n1v4GANwTc4USPrF0GWKWcQzZyEZfklgUH-npYOd3tPK52bonRRCTPLI>, a user profile<https://c.gle/AOExmq1_TC5lNtaCUvVB_nmapLIc6XC0kdrx0PO22wAa05HizHfM02EfP3IsmyuHZycyKKZjBvVs546JO4sDZGYaNihdU9D1m7BSpF_Rbchjcb4Zl2OwRCCsut1sS845FWXuMGt8wlseb9uhx_3wxkdqdNyYPBjYVRhoHcpY5zg5yWzHXwsbhCejvUzJ3g>, or a business listing<https://c.gle/AOExmq35CTx3k3g6HFOVRGwYJuyzsM6zZ3vWNdx6HRHGbyJORorq_0XJbRLtU-ouf_iqUajxc9T3fvo8ygJ2AYDRq_UJehUCucoybPlSyGthMZhpw5ek7JzNmd48klgqB-IZzLqxDH5-QuhrP3I1Al_c4szClX4fjUGelkamOzMPcZdT_5d6RE2NSiYlIw> that you believe may be violating our policies, please report it.\n\n• Beware of scams: Watch out for messages or phone calls from people you don’t know offering money in exchange for leaving a review. Learn more<https://c.gle/AOExmq2dYkjKXr7NBlukc6LkzbZA2tgwmLciKFrpDJ9S1igoJi-qlO8qtHwjSI3AOsneocFBtHXtG-5S_7PqV-yKvqsEa3YUNQqeCWQ9D3zillNuia6QqXJRX9drs-JEr5huoBVksH7tcUDrLUpFW_cskUDShhRhE6RPdqeIwv8anI04hGygjQ>\n\nThank you,\n\nThe Google Maps Team\n\n\nYou received this email because you contributed content to Google Maps.\n\n(c) 2026 Google LLC 1600 Amphitheatre Parkway, Mountain View, CA 94043','incoming-complaint','INCOMING',NULL,'2026-06-21 16:52:16',NULL,NULL,NULL),('a975e2f4-6633-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7','d69e6b3d-662d-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Dear Google Maps,\n\nWe have received your ticket regarding: \"Google Maps Policy Reminder\".\n\nYour reference ID is #d69e6b3d. This issue has been logged and is currently under review by our team.','approved-draft-placeholder','INCOMING',NULL,'2026-06-21 16:52:16',NULL,NULL,NULL),('a98134c0-6633-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7','d69e6b3d-662d-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'The Atlas\n\nBy GenAI Works\n\n\nBy GenAI Works\n\n    \n    \n    Read this article on LinkedIn to join the conversation\nRead on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/why-openais-gpt-55-failed-financial-analyst-exam-genai-works-gfr0f?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BS0bRP08%2BSUWIvlQcICgutg%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=029RpK1L3YoYg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_-null-icq8ji~mp7afoo5~uy-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mp7afoo5-uy\n  \nWelcome back! OpenAI\'s GPT-5.5 failed nearly half of a junior financial analyst exam, raising hard questions about how fast AI can really replace knowledge workers. At the same time, the enterprise AI market also shifted at the top, with Anthropic overtaking OpenAI in paid US business adoption for the first time since ChatGPT launched. Meanwhile, OpenAI is forcing every macOS user to update four apps before June 12 after attackers reached internal signing certificates in a supply chain attack. And while the competitive race intensifies, the Gates Foundation committed $200 million alongside Anthropic for public-good AI in health and education. In today\'s Generative AI Newsletter: The AI finance reality check: Why did OpenAI’s GPT-5.5 fail half a junior financial analyst exam despite being one of the most advanced AI models on the market? The enterprise AI crossover: How did Anthropic overtake OpenAI in paid US business adoption for the first time since ChatGPT launched? OpenAI’s June 12 deadline: What exactly happened inside the macOS certificate breach that forced OpenAI to rotate every signing key it owns? Gates’ second AI bet: Why is the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation now funding two frontier AI labs at the same time, and what does that mean for the future of public AI infrastructure? OpenAI\'s GPT-5.5 failed half a junior financial analyst exam If you\'re relying on AI financial agents to handle your due diligence, they just failed tasks that many junior analysts are expected to pass. This month, OpenAI’s GPT-5.5 was tested on tasks normally done by junior financial analysts. The model answered fewer than 52% of the questions correctly. The results come from the May 2026 release of Vals AI Finance Agent v2, a benchmark used to measure how close AI models are to replacing entry-level finance analysts. The benchmark is much harder than older finance AI tests because it measures complete task execution, not simple one-question trivia.  The models must complete real multi-step financial work like extracting figures, running calculations, and connecting financial conclusions, instead of only answering isolated prompts. The benchmark uses tasks pulled directly from real equity research, credit analysis, and corporate finance workflows. The results were close: GPT-5.5 slightly outperformed Anthropic’s Claude and Google’s Gemini All three frontier models scored roughly in the high-40% to low-50% range Older GPT-4-class systems scored around 30-40% on comparable work two years ago Progress is still happening, but far more slowly than many AI marketing claims suggest. And inside a real finance firm, 52% accuracy is still unusable. This level of performance would raise serious red flags, and firms may need to think twice before replacing another analyst seat with AI. GenAI.Works Crowdfunding Round is Live Many of you have been part of GenAI Works from the beginning. What started as AI content is now a $2M+ revenue business with strong growth: 2.4x annual revenue growth 300+ paying customers (NVIDIA, Google, IBM, ElevenLabs) 14M+ audience driving distribution Now we’re entering the next stage, which is building our own AI products. GenAI Academy already has 30K users, our 9 hackathons drew 28K participants and led us to build the Hackathon App, and ToneUp powers our 14M+ content machine. We’ve now opened a crowdfunding round to scale product development and expansion. CTA: Join until July 1 and earn up to 25% extra shares.  In making an investment decision, investors must rely on their own examination of the issuer and the terms of the offering, including the merits and risks involved. Genai Works, Inc. has filed a Form C with the Securities and Exchange Commission in connection with its offering, a copy of which may be obtained here:https://bit.ly/3APlUkJ Why Enterprise AI Still Fails Most companies are investing heavily in AI, yet measurable business impact remains limited. Enterprise data is still fragmented across disconnected systems, making it difficult for AI agents to maintain reliable context and operate consistently at scale. On May 18 at 10 AM ET, Ravi Marwaha, Chief Operating Officer and Chief Product & Technology Officer at Arango, joins Steve Nouri, CEO of GenAI.Works, to unpack why AI projects stall between prototype and production. Drawing from leadership roles at JPMorgan, SAP, and Informatica, Ravi will share how trusted business context can support compliance, operational response, dynamic pricing, and enterprise decision-making across complex environments. Join here Anthropic passes OpenAI on business adoption After nearly three years of OpenAI dominance, Anthropic has officially taken the lead in paid US business adoption. According to Ramp’s May AI Index, Anthropic climbed to 34.4% of American companies in April, overtaking OpenAI’s 32.3%. The bigger story is the speed behind the change. For the first time since ChatGPT launched, the AI business race has flipped. Anthropic quadrupled its business footprint over the past year while OpenAI barely moved, helped by the rapid expansion of Claude into startups and small businesses through its new “Claude for Small Business” offering. After dominating the market from 2023 through mid-2025, OpenAI appears stuck around the 30% mark while competitors continue accelerating underneath it. And all of this is happening while the entire AI economy is exploding. More than half of US businesses now pay for at least one AI product, up from only 5% in early 2023. The market is expanding so quickly that leadership changes are happening while the entire industry is still doubling in size. Even though Anthropic is currently leading the enterprise AI race, that advantage could change very quickly as pricing pressures begin to grow. For example, Uber’s CTO revealed last month that the company had already exhausted its entire 2026 AI budget on Claude Code usage, and that was before Anthropic’s latest pricing announcements, which further increased costs for image-related prompts and advanced model usage. The next few months may decide whether April was simply a temporary shift or the beginning of a much larger power transition in enterprise AI. What do you think: will Anthropic still be leading the enterprise AI market by the end of 2026? Every OpenAI macOS apps must be updated by June 12 OpenAI has given every macOS user a hard deadline to update ChatGPT Desktop, the Codex App, Codex CLI, and Atlas before June 12, 2026. After that date, the old code-signing certificate will be fully revoked. Any app still signed with it may be blocked by macOS security protections and fail to launch. iOS and Windows users do not need to take any action.  So, what exactly happened behind the scenes that forced OpenAI to rotate every macOS signing key it owns?  On May 11, the open-source library TanStack was compromised during the “Mini Shai-Hulud” supply chain attack. Malicious npm packages infected two employee devices inside OpenAI’s corporate environment. Attackers gained access to a limited number of internal source code repositories. Those repositories contained signing certificates used for OpenAI applications. So, OpenAI decided to rotate the affected certificates and force macOS users to update before June 12. Step back for a moment, and this may be one of the clearest real-world examples yet of the coming AI-versus-AI cybersecurity field, summarized perfectly by the CEO of Palo Alto Networks Nikesh Arora in four words: “AI has to fight AI.” Which leads to a difficult question you should probably ask right now: If the same attack hit your company tomorrow, what would the cert rotation, customer notifications, forced app updates and lost-trust cost actually add up to on your balance sheet? Anthropic and the Gates Foundation Commit $200 Million for public-good AI   What if the future of AI in healthcare and education is no longer controlled by a handful of Silicon Valley companies? That may be the bigger story behind the new $200 million partnership between the Bill & Melinda Gates Foundation and Anthropic. Less than a year after backing OpenAI, the Gates Foundation has now committed to a second major AI lab.  The four-year initiative with Anthropic targets public-good AI in health, education and agriculture across low- and middle-income countries and underserved US communities. But the structure of the deal matters as much as the money itself. Anthropic will contribute: Claude usage credits Engineering support Technical infrastructure Meanwhile, the Gates Foundation will provide: Grant funding Program design expertise Access to its global health and development partner network Together, the goal is to create shared AI infrastructure that researchers and public institutions can actually use and AI applications designed for problems frontier labs rarely prioritize. Still, operational detail is missing: no concrete roadmap, no delivery timeline, no quantified milestones. Which means the real test will begin when the first artifacts are released later this year. Let’s wait and see. Everything else you shouldn’t miss: $4B for AI that builds AI: Ex-Google, Meta and OpenAI researchers launched Recursive Superintelligence, a $4 billion bet on AI that builds better AI. SpacexAI cofounder lines up his own billion: SpacexAI cofounder Igor Babuschkin is in talks to raise up to $1 billion for a new AI research startup, River AI, at a valuation of up to $5 billion, sources told Forbes. Cerebras cashes in on the chip rush: AI chipmaker Cerebras raised $5.55 billion in the year\'s biggest IPO, pricing shares at $185 for a $40 billion valuation after orders hit 20 times the supply. What does it actually mean? What changes? How could this improve AI for companies like OpenAI and Anthropic if they work with them?  The economics of superstar AI researchers: Superstar AI researchers are paid >10× more than their frontier lab colleagues, and >100× more than most postdocs. OpenAI lawyers up against Apple: OpenAI is considering legal action against Apple regarding their two-year-old partnership, with its legal team evaluating options ranging from a breach-of-contract notice to potentially broader legal measures short of a full lawsuit. Work with codex from anywhere: Codex now runs from anywhere, including iOS, Slack and the web, with cloud tasks handing off to local sessions mid-flow. Learn more about AI from the experts building it 📸 Check our Instagram for AI news in 30 seconds  🐝 Check our GenAI for weekly expert insights  📺 Check our YouTube to learn from the people building AI  🐦 Check our X for AI news as it lands  🧠 Learn how to build your next AI application PS: Want to share your AI tool or company news with our 13M+ community? Get in touch.\nKeep reading on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/why-openais-gpt-55-failed-financial-analyst-exam-genai-works-gfr0f?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BS0bRP08%2BSUWIvlQcICgutg%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=029RpK1L3YoYg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_-null-icq8ji~mp7afoo5~uy-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mp7afoo5-uy\n  \n      \n\n----------------------------------------\n\nThis email was intended for Yashica Lohar (Flipkart GWC 7.0 Scholar | CSE ’28 @ IIIT Jabalpur | 2 ⭐ @Codechef)\nLearn why we included this: https://www.linkedin.com/help/linkedin/answer/4788?lang=en&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BS0bRP08%2BSUWIvlQcICgutg%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=029RpK1L3YoYg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer-null-icq8ji~mp7afoo5~uy-null-