mmer&trkEmail=eml-email_accept_invite_single_01-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer-null-icq8ji~mpe2c9ph~fc-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mpe2c9ph-fc&otpToken=MTMwNDFlZTkxNTI5YzljMmJjMjgwZmViNDExOWVlYjY4N2NmZDY0MjliYWM4ZTZiN2JjZjA1Njc0NzU5NTRmYmYwZDBkM2U5NDRmOWUyZGE2MWEzZDJjNTY4MTg4MzI2ZTM0ZDQxM2FkYTMwOTAyNzhkYzRjNWNlLDEsMQ%3D%3D\nAccepted invitation\n\n\nUnsubscribe: https://www.linkedin.com/comm/psettings/email-unsubscribe?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_accept_invite_single_01%3BpaWvkL7oSnG9H42HpaPkPQ%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=1IIBLzVi6BvIg1&trk=eml-email_accept_invite_single_01-unsubscribe-0-textfooterglimmer&trkEmail=eml-email_accept_invite_single_01-unsubscribe-0-textfooterglimmer-null-icq8ji~mpe2c9ph~fc-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mpe2c9ph-fc&loid=AQEkAzL7mixTaAAAAZ5Fcxu8yoDNoGxo83jCdvO3Ta9a7zWonSfGXMAv7wKsnXr6CHWJK5FMlpfvejNgmdDjrRCCF-QNlRmvNdo0WfmVyFpF\nHelp: https://www.linkedin.com/help/linkedin/answer/67?lang=en&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_accept_invite_single_01%3BpaWvkL7oSnG9H42HpaPkPQ%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=1IIBLzVi6BvIg1&trk=eml-email_accept_invite_single_01-help-0-textfooterglimmer&trkEmail=eml-email_accept_invite_single_01-help-0-textfooterglimmer-null-icq8ji~mpe2c9ph~fc-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mpe2c9ph-fc&otpToken=MTMwNDFlZTkxNTI5YzljMmJjMjgwZmViNDExOWVlYjY4N2NmZDY0MjliYWM4ZTZiN2JjZjA1Njc0NzU5NTRmYmYwZDBkM2U5NDRmOWUyZGE2MWEzZDJjNTY4MTg4MzI2ZTM0ZDQxM2FkYTMwOTAyNzhkYzRjNWNlLDEsMQ%3D%3D\n\n© 2026 LinkedIn Corporation, 1zwnj000 West Maude Avenue, Sunnyvale, CA 94085.\nLinkedIn and the LinkedIn logo are registered trademarks of LinkedIn.','incoming-complaint','INCOMING',NULL,'2026-06-21 16:52:16',NULL,NULL,NULL),('aa2bdc02-6633-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7','d69e6b3d-662d-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Dear Nainika Agrawal via LinkedIn,\n\nWe have received your ticket regarding: \"Nainika  accepted your invitation, explore their network\".\n\nYour reference ID is #d69e6b3d. This issue has been logged and is currently under review by our team.','approved-draft-placeholder','INCOMING',NULL,'2026-06-21 16:52:16',NULL,NULL,NULL),('aa355e2d-6633-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7','d69e6b3d-662d-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'LinkedIn 360 l By GaryVee\n\nBy Gary Vaynerchuk\n\n\nBy Gary Vaynerchuk\n\n    \n    \n    Read this article on LinkedIn to join the conversation\nRead on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/why-youre-meant-same-person-all-time-gary-vaynerchuk-umdke?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3Bw8CteAtbTMKDZufG2xZxHA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=01HYhgsHs3vYg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_-null-icq8ji~mpe5nfs8~8l-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mpe5nfs8-8l\n  \nEarlier this year, before the year really got going, I jotted down a few ideas in a notebook. I’ve done that here and there over the years, usually on vacation. Sometimes on a long flight, occasionally when I don’t have access to a device. Sometimes, I feel myself adding a little more structure to how I operate. At the start of 2026, I wrote down a few mantas for this season of my life: Intention and impact. I want everything this year to be around these two ideas. But whatever your mantra is, or your goals are, we need to be talking about seasons in life a lot more. I think we’re not understanding winter, spring, fall, and summer well enough. It’s kind of how I think about life. It’s kind of how I think about my year. Right now, as I just talked earlier, I’m fully intentional and very, very focused. But I have a funny feeling—and my team knows this—after we scratch that itch, we’re going to be in a free-for-all creativity bubble, freedom world, once I feel like it. I like to yin and yang. I like to adjust. People become too ideological or rigid. They stick with one thing and they’re like, “This is the way it’s supposed to be” without adjusting to the reality. My friends: Changing your mind based on the context of the current situation is a strength, not a weakness. It’s just the truth…and with decisions, seasons, anything… you don’t know the alternative and you never will. There’s no time machine, there’s no future machine, there’s just no way to really know whether you made the right move or not. Whichever decision you make, you won’t know how the other one would’ve played out. Just flow with life, flow with the season you’re in. Be Where You Are You shouldn’t go hard 24/7. I’m a fan of sleep. I’m a fan of vacation. I’m a fan of checking out. When you’re off, be off. When you’re on, be on. People are flabbergasted when I tell them that I sleep seven or eight hours a day.. A lot of people know me as like, “I go hard” ... but the reality is I will sleep 10 hours a day if I need to. I listen to my body very well. I never thought not sleeping was gonna make me successful ... it’s more about while I’m awake, I need to be productive and make sh*t happen. But my friends.. don’t put going hard on a pedestal. Work when you work and rest when you rest. Everybody thinks they can have the perfect decision. There is no perfect decision. There’s just life. I’m just completely confused by people’s lack of ability to understand that you still have time. You have to change the season you’re in. Maybe now you’re working a lot, and that’s OK. Maybe later you’ll be resting, and that’s OK too, friends. I’m blown away by people’s lack of understanding of how long life is; how long the game is. I push patience in life because I know life is long. Everybody around here is running around like it’s not. 24 year-olds running around like it ends tomorrow. Like they need it now. What’s wrong with being 26 or 41 or 73? Life Is Just One Season After the Next To me, breaking up life into seasons is just a natural feeling of being a human being. You can feel when you’ve hit a point where there was momentum, a changing opinion or vibe or part of your life, and you are now days, weeks, or sometimes a couple months into a new era, and that’s just how it’s always worked out for me. Just being a human being, being in touch with my feelings, not being scared of my feelings, not pushing against my feelings, not trying to create an ideology for my feelings, just being with my actual feelings. I feel like multiple seasons can coexist because there’s multiple parts of your life—professional, personal, even above that, spiritual. You can be in different places and different parts of your life at the same time. You Define Your Life, Seasons, and Happiness Seasons of life is also about having your own definition of joy, happiness, and what life you want to live. If you make $57,000 a year and have the work-life balance that you want and you’re happy as sh*t, you f*cking won. I know people making $8 million a year that are f*cking miserable and hate their life… and I have friends who make $67,000 a year, loving life .. four softball teams and love it. I don’t give a f*ck how much money you make. Do you feel pumped as f*ck when you wake up?? We have to redefine success and dramatically start downplaying money and start playing up happiness. If you want this season of life to be all about family, great. Another season, maybe it’s all about work. Also great. You get to decide. Infusing Structure and “Impact and Intentionality” I always have a lot of different things going on. So, I feel I need a new season this year: I needed impact and intentionality. Specifically, I think some of my long-tenured employees needed clarity on if they were bringing success, not just, you know, vibing in the halls being like, “keep up the good work.” Between VaynerMedia, VaynerSports, VeeFriends, VaynerX, Wine Text, and Wine Library. Like, I have a lot of businesses quote-unquote going well, but the challenge is maintaining that, keeping it up. It’s kind of like a head coach with a football team. I think a lot of head coaches like to have a motto for the year, right? Or like a chant—like you see that a little bit in culture. I would say I’m treating it that way. Like it gets brought up in the huddle, but it’s not like in every turn or every practice or every game I’m repeating that sentence over and over. It’s in the air. I talk about it in some meetings. Seasons of Life and Optimal Speed As for seasons more broadly, though, I think my optimal speed is go, go, go. I mean like during my kids’ spring break, we went to Miami. I’m with them right now as I write this. They’re—you know, seven, eight years ago when they were waking up at 8:30 in the morning, this would not be happening, right? But because I have teenage kids now, they may not wake up until noon. I think they woke up at 1:30 p.m. the other day. So, I’m good at maximizing—like for me, this is a perfect example on a vacation with kids that are going to sleep ‘til noon. If they’re sleeping a lot, I’ll use that morning time to do work or meetings. Being in Miami as a New York businessman, this is a very common window for like a guy to go golfing or, if I was a different type of dude, for fishing morning or, at bare minimum, like walking on the beach for two or three hours, right? You know, for me, it’s more fun to make content, meet with colleagues, or build something special for the business. I’ve been talking about this idea for 20 years, of not living for the weekend, but making sure you enjoy your work. That’s so important, because you spend so much of your time in life working. The “Bridge” Philosophy: Caring with Detachment Sometimes, I misdiagnose a season. It happens. Maybe I’ll wake up in six years and realize I had misdiagnosed this season, or maybe I was wishing I was in a different season of life. Who knows? But the utter detachment from the seriousness of it all is is so real. And I know you’ve heard this from me. I’ll say it this way: if you played Uno or Monopoly or ping-pong all day and you were hyper-competitive, you want to win so bad. That’s how I am. But I know it doesn’t mean anything. I think at some level professionally, that’s how I feel. When you’re aware that it doesn’t really matter, it just takes all the heat off. I mean again, I’ve traveled a little bit here, and I work non-stop, but I’m pretty chill about it all in the scheme of things. In other words, I don’t view anything professionally as mission critical. If all the money was taken out of the bank account and our company went out of business tomorrow, I still wouldn’t feel like it defines my life. So I’m able to be incredibly calm when things are bad, because I don’t take anything personally. And I feel like it’s my responsibility to be strong for the room. I really think if I do an autobiography as my ultimate final book, if I write that kind of book, it is just going to be called The Bridge. I’m pulling some hard from both sides, on extreme offense in life and business, but also extreme chill and making sure we’re having a lot fun along the way.\nKeep reading on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/why-youre-meant-same-person-all-time-gary-vaynerchuk-umdke?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3Bw8CteAtbTMKDZufG2xZxHA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=01HYhgsHs3vYg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_-null-icq8ji~mpe5nfs8~8l-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mpe5nfs8-8l\n  \n      \n\n----------------------------------------\n\nThis email was intended for Yashica Lohar (Flipkart GWC 7.0 Scholar | CSE ’28 @ IIIT Jabalpur | 2 ⭐ @Codechef)\nLearn why we included this: https://www.linkedin.com/help/linkedin/answer/4788?lang=en&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3Bw8CteAtbTMKDZufG2xZxHA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=01HYhgsHs3vYg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_news