accessible only to venture funds, strategic investors, and large corporate partners. That may be changing. The three giants of the artificial intelligence sector Anthropic, OpenAl, and SpaceX, are rushing to test investor appetite in the stock markets. SpaceX has reportedly filed IPO paperwork, while OpenAI and Anthropic are also being watched as potential public-market candidates. If these companies move toward public listings, it could mark one of the biggest shifts in technology investing since the dot-com era. Once these companies enter public markets, the story changes. Investors will no longer judge them only by vision, growth, and ambition. They will start asking harder questions: Can these companies show real margins? Can they control infrastructure costs? Can they explain their governance structures? Can founder control survive public-market pressure? That is where the real test begins when AI starts moving from private hype to public pricing. And when that happens, the market will not only value the dream. It will value the numbers behind it. Subscribe here to learn why they’re going public. Don’t miss it. New partnership between GenAI Works and Oracle. The company is now raising its next round.  Oracle is now a strategic partner of GenAI Works. Together we\'re running hackathons, hosting AI trend conversations on Oracle TV, and building Oracle into the activities that move our 14M+ community of AI professionals, founders and builders. Not a sponsor logo. A working partnership across content, events, and community. Oracle joins a client list that includes Google, IBM, Nvidia, ElevenLabs and Bright Data. 300+ enterprise and startup clients in total. The company behind those relationships grew revenue from $1M in 2024 to $2.5M in 2025, a 2.5x increase in one year. 14M+ community. Zero long-term debt. GenAI Works is raising its next round. Investments start from just $1,000. Commit by July 1, 2026 to qualify for up to 25% bonus shares. Invest now Binance’s $10B AI Fraud Shield Binance says it prevented more than $10 billion in fraud using 100+ AI models over the past year. From Q1 2025 to Q1 2026, Binance says its AI systems helped protect 5.4M+ users and intercepted 22.9M scam and phishing attempts. In Q1 2026 alone, the company says it helped protect around $1.98B in user funds. The scale is significant:  36,000+ malicious addresses blacklisted 9,600+ real-time warnings issued daily 179,000+ users reached through account takeover education Binance says this approach combines AI deployment, real-time monitoring, user education, and secure-by-design architecture. What is the result? AI is becoming a financial security layer. But there is another side to this story. The same AI tooling that blocks fraud also creates one of the most sensitive behavioral datasets in private hands. More than 100 models scoring millions of users in real time means Binance is building a massive behavioral graph of crypto activity. That raises many questions: What are the false-positive rates? How often are accounts frozen incorrectly? How is this behavioral data governed? How often do adversarial AI systems bypass these defenses? And how much of this data could become relevant to regulators or governments? For Binance, the $10 billion blocked is the headline. For the rest of the crypto industry, it may become the new benchmark. What do you think: Will AI become essential to protecting Bitcoin holders and digital assets, or will it also create a new layer of financial surveillance? How is SpaceX selling AI compute at scale through Anthropic? Beyond rockets, SpaceX is now  selling AI compute “at significant scale,” and Anthropic is the proof point. According to Benzinga\'s report, Anthropic agreed to pay SpaceX $1.25 billion per month through May 2029 for compute capacity across SpaceX’s Colossus and Colossus II AI training clusters. Musk also said SpaceX is already in discussions with other companies to offer similar services. This means the Anthropic deal may not be a one-off agreement. It could be the beginning of a new business line where SpaceX sells compute capacity to AI companies that need massive infrastructure to train and run advanced AI models.  The main story is SpaceX positioning itself as part of the AI infrastructure race. As AI models demand more power, cooling, land, and compute capacity, Musk is pushing an even bigger idea: orbital data centers. The logic is simple: Earth-based data centers are constrained by electricity, cooling, and real estate. Space-based data centers could theoretically use solar power, satellite infrastructure, and orbital scale to support future AI workloads. But the challenge is big. Orbital data centers still face serious questions around cost, latency, maintenance, reliability, regulation, and commercial viability. What do you think?  Can SpaceX turn orbital data centers from a bold vision into a profitable AI infrastructure business? If it can, SpaceX may become one of the companies powering the next generation of AI. AI could automate up to 50% of financial services work  In addition to changing tools in financial services, AI is starting to change the workforce itself. A new UK government-commissioned report warns that AI could automate between 30% and 50% of tasks in most financial services roles.  The report, titled A Workforce Transformed, was produced by the Financial Services Skills Commission after the UK government asked for an assessment of AI skills, training needs, and innovation in the sector. The message is that many jobs will be redesigned. Agentic AI could put pressure on entry-level roles, especially the tasks traditionally used to train junior employees. If those tasks are automated, firms will need new pathways for people to enter and grow inside the sector. The talent challenge is also getting bigger. By 2035, the UK financial services sector could lose up to 450,000 of its 780,000 highly skilled workers through retirement or turnover. At the same time, demand for AI, data, and technology skills is increasing across the whole economy. That means financial institutions may face two problems at once. AI will automate parts of today’s workforce, while the sector also struggles to replace the talent it is losing. So, if firms use AI only to reduce headcount, the employment impact could be severe.  But if they use it to grow, redesign roles, train workers, and create new services, AI could become a productivity engine instead of just a workforce shock. The next phase of financial services will be defined by how well firms manage the transition from human-only workflows to AI-augmented work. Everything else you shouldn’t miss: Meta Layoffs Spark AI Job Loss Debate: Meta cut 8,000 workers (about 10% of staff) and reassigned 7,000 more to new AI teams, prompting Bernie Sanders to ask publicly what Zuckerberg\'s AI will do to the average American worker if it can already replace one in ten of his own employees. Anthropic on Track for First Profitable Quarter: Anthropic is on track to post its first profitable quarter, expecting $10.9 billion in Q2 2026 revenue and $559 million in operating profit. The surge is driven by strong enterprise demand for its Claude AI models, though the company may not remain profitable in future quarters as it ramps up computing costs. Benzinga wants to be financial AI\'s data layer: Benzinga turned its real-time financial news, market data and SEC filings into AI-native APIs built for LLMs and RAG systems, with a stated bet to become the default data layer for financial AI. Huawei wants banks to hand AI the keys: Huawei launched its 150-partner RONGHAI Program at the Shanghai Intelligent Finance Summit this week, pitching banks on agentic AI that handles payments, risk monitoring and routine decisions without human approval. Learn more about AI from the experts building it 📸 Follow us on Instagram for fast, visual AI updates in 30 seconds.  🐝 Subscribe to our Generative AI daily newsletter — trusted by 120 000+ subscribers — to stay ahead of the latest AI news across tech, education, and business.  📺 Watch us on YouTube to hear insights directly from leading AI voices, builders, and innovators. 🐦 Follow us on X for breaking AI news and real-time industry updates. 🧠 Learn how to build your next AI application with practical resources and expert guidance. 🚀 Explore investment opportunities in the future of AI and join our community-backed growth journey. PS: Want to share your AI tool or company news with our 14M+ community? Get in touch.\nKeep reading on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/who-first-land-ai-public-market-genai-works-mrbtf?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BTfSF9AclRNuC%2Fni6VcF0CA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=2kPaTxVd9ryYg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_-null-icq8ji~mphai86i~j2-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mphai86i-j2\n  \n      \n\n----------------------------------------\n\nThis email was intended for Yashica Lohar (Flipkart GWC 7.0 Scholar | CSE ’28 @ IIIT Jabalpur | 2 ⭐ @Codechef)\nLearn why we included this: https://www.linkedin.com/help/linkedin/answer/4788?lang=en&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BTfSF9AclRNuC%2Fni6VcF0CA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=2kPaTxVd9ryYg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer-null-icq8ji~mphai86i~j2-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mphai86i-j2\nYou are receiving LinkedIn notification emails. Others can see that you are a subscriber.\n\n\nUnsubscribe: https://www.linkedin.com/series-notifications/?action=unsubscribe&memberToken=ADoAAEIlwb4B7wSObgzu320aKxhPrfHhU2UtxwY&newsletterId=7075090904906149891&newsletterTitle=The Atlas&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_e