l-icq8ji~mplku1h6~fs-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mplku1h6-fs\n  \nWelcome back! This week, Anthropic\'s most capable AI model quietly exposed a flaw in how the entire software industry handles security. On another update, hidden Claude Code feature surfaced before its official announcement and then vanished. Anthropic’s new joiner Karpathy has a framework for making AI coding actually work, and it has hit 82,000 GitHub stars. And, DeepSeek just rewrote the pricing rules for enterprise AI. A lot moved this week.  In today\'s Atlas Newsletter: Anthropic\'s AI found 10,000 critical vulnerabilities in 30 days: Claude Mythos Preview and 50 partners exposed the part of cybersecurity that AI hasn\'t fixed yet. Claude Code hid something worth watching: A workflows tool for multi-agent orchestration appeared in the codebase, then disappeared. Anthropic pulled it via a server-side flag. That looks like a staged release, and next week we\'ll show you how to use it. The 4 principles that make Claude Code actually work: The CEO of Google Sundar Pichai says a solo developer with Claude can outcompete a 10-person Google team. Karpathy\'s CLAUDE.md, now at 82,000 GitHub stars, is the setup that makes that true. What happens when DeepSeek becomes 75% cheaper: DeepSeek made its price cut permanent, putting V4 Pro at $268 to run a full intelligence benchmark. GPT-5.5 costs 12 times more for the same test. The AI model race now has a different scoreboard. PS: Want to share your AI tool or company news with our 14M+ community? Get in touch. How did Anthropic\'s AI find 10,000 software security issues in 30 days? In less than a month, Anthropic’s AI and around 50 partners surfaced over 10,000 high-severity software security. Cloudflare found 2,000 bugs across its critical systems. Mozilla found 271 vulnerabilities in Firefox 150, more than ten times what it found in Firefox 148 using Claude Opus 4.6. Also, several partners reported that their bug-finding rate increased by a factor of ten or more. Can you guess how? They used Claude Mythos Preview. And the burden has shifted beyond discovery. It now sits in verification, disclosure, patching, and deployment.  That change creates a specific risk: the same capability accelerating defense can also compress the time and cost required for offense. Anthropic has not released Mythos-class models publicly, citing the need for stronger safeguards before general release. But similar capabilities will eventually reach the market from other labs. The window between vulnerability discovery and patched deployment is where attacks happen.  And that window is becoming harder to manage with traditional patch cycles. Anthropic has also released Claude Security in public beta for enterprise customers, a tool that scans codebases and generates proposed fixes. In three weeks, it helped patch more than 2,100 vulnerabilities. Takeaway: Cybersecurity’s new bottleneck surpasses finding the weakness and lands on acting on it fast enough. What do you think?  New partnership between GenAI Works and Oracle. The company is now raising its next round Oracle joined GenAI Works in April 2026 to grow brand recognition through the creator network. A joint hackathon is also in the works. That\'s one of 300+ clients, including Google, IBM and Nvidia, inside a 14M+ AI community. The company behind those relationships grew revenue from $1M in 2024 to $2.5M in 2025. 2.5x in one year. GenAI Works has opened a new crowdfunding round. Investments start from only $1,000. Commit by July 1 for up to 25% bonus shares. Invest today Organizations like Adobe and MIT cracked the AI trust code. Want to know their secret? Most AI chatbots hallucinate. They make stuff up because they\'re trained on the entire internet. Your customers and team ask questions, get answers that sound right but aren\'t, and suddenly your brand loses credibility. Sounds familiar? What if your AI only answered from the information you gave it? That\'s CustomGPT.ai. It grounds every response in your approved content, cites every source and admits when it doesn\'t know something. You can see, and review, every answer.  MIT built ChatMTC with it. Adobe, Dropbox and the UN chose it too. They all needed AI they could trust to represent them. Ready to build an AI your customers and team can rely on? Create your demo in 3 mins. Claude Code hid something worth watching A new feature appeared inside Claude Code before Anthropic was ready to talk about it.  A workflows tool for deterministic multi-agent orchestration showed up in the codebase, then disappeared.  Anthropic pulled the reference from the changelog and switched it off via a server-side flag. That looks less like a cancellation and more like a staged release waiting for an announcement. Indeed, the feature solves a real problem that anyone running multi-agent tasks in Claude Code has likely faced.  Until now, the standard approach has routed every sub-agent result back through the main orchestrator’s context window before passing it to the next agent.  Run ten or fifteen agents in sequence and the orchestrator starts to degrade. It forgets earlier context. It makes weaker decisions as the task gets longer. And, the token costs compound with every round trip. The Workflows tool changes that model. Instead of relying on the main model as the orchestrator, the workflow is controlled through a JavaScript file. Results pass directly between agents without touching the main context window.  That means teams can chain 20, 50 or 100 agents and the orchestrator stays clean.  Loops, conditionals, parallel batching, structured output schemas and token budgets all become native features of the workflow rather than fragile instructions inside a prompt. Subscribe to get a list of tips and tricks on how to be ready to use /workflows in Claude Code and Claude Cowork here. Don\'t miss it. 4 principles that make Claude Code succeed The CEO of Google Sundar Pichai said that a “solo developer using Claude can now outcompete a 10-person Google team.” That sounds like a bold claim. But it points to a real change: AI coding tools are becoming powerful enough to change the economics of software development. The problem is that most developers are not using them in a way that unlocks that potential. Many Claude Code users still start every session from zero, and the result is catastrophic. Andrej Karpathy’s CLAUDE.md is a simple fix for this. It turns AI coding from a blank chat session into a structured engineering workflow. The file is built around four principles: Think Before Coding: The model must state assumptions, identify ambiguity, and ask before guessing. Simplicity First: The model should write the minimum code needed to solve the problem. No speculative features. No unnecessary abstractions. Surgical Changes: The model should only edit what was requested. Adjacent code, comments, and formatting should stay untouched unless required. Goal-Driven Execution: Instead of giving the model vague commands, give it clear success criteria. Karpathy summarized the idea well: “Don’t tell it what to do, give it success criteria and watch it go.” That is the real lesson. The productivity gap is about how much context, structure, and discipline you give the model before it starts coding. Pichai’s claim may be possible. But only for developers who stop treating AI coding tools like autocomplete and start treating them like structured engineering agents. The setup takes minutes. The difference can be massive. What happens when DeepSeek becomes 75% cheaper? DeepSeek V4 Pro became the world’s best value AI model after the company made its 75% API price cut permanent. That changes how the AI model race is being scored. At its new official API rate, running Artificial Analysis’ full Intelligence Index benchmark on V4 Pro costs $268. The same test on OpenAI’s GPT-5.5 costs roughly 12 times more. On Anthropic’s Claude Opus 4.7, it costs about 19 times more. That gap is large enough to change buying behavior. And that is the point. Chinese AI companies have spent the last 18 months competing on a different axis from their US counterparts. While OpenAI and Anthropic compete on raw capability benchmarks, enterprise features, and ecosystem depth, DeepSeek, MiniMax, Xiaomi, and Alibaba are pushing the intelligence-per-dollar race. Alibaba is now following the same playbook with Qwen3.7 Max, launched with a 50% promotional price cut through June 22. DeepSeek also said in April that prices could fall further once Huawei’s Ascend 950PR supernodes ship at scale. Making the current promotional rate permanent signals confidence that the cost structure can support this long term. For developers and enterprise teams, the implication is direct: At these prices, choosing a US flagship model over a Chinese one must be justified by capability, compliance, trust, data governance, or ecosystem requirements. For many use cases, cost-performance may become the deciding factor. The AI model race is about who can deliver the most intelligence per dollar. What do you think? Will intelligence-per-dollar become the new benchmark for enterprise AI adoption? Everything else you shouldn’t miss: Spotify adds AI-Generated podcasts and playlists for premium users: Spotify is giving premium users AI-generated personal podcasts, custom playlists and audiobook picks tailored to their listening history. Disney faces $5M lawsuit over facial recognition at theme parks: Disney is facing a $5M class-action lawsuit over claims its new facial-recognition system at California theme parks collects biometric data from guests without their consent. Cursor hits $3 billion annualized revenue ahead of SpaceX deal: Cursor\'s annualized revenue hit $3 billion in late April, making it one of the fastest-growing startups on record, with SpaceX holding a $60 billion acquisition option that opens shortly after its expected June 12 IPO. Manus seeks $1 billion to reverse Meta acquisition: China\'s regulators have ordered Manus to reverse its Meta acquisition, pushing founders into rare territory: raising up to $1 billion to buy back a deal that had already closed. Microsoft in talks to supply Maia AI chips to Anthropic: Microsoft is in talks to supply its Maia AI chips to Anthropic following a $5 billion investment, with Maia\'s 30% performance improvement aimed at easing Anthropic\'s compute constraints. Learn more about AI from the experts building it 📸 Follow us on Instagram for fast, visual AI updates in 30 seconds.  🐝 Subscribe to our Generative AI daily newsletter — trusted by 120 000+ subscribers — to stay ahead of the latest AI news across tech, education, and business.  📺 Watch us on YouTube to hear insights directly from leading AI voices, builders, and innovators. 🐦 Follow us on X for breaking AI news and real-time industry updates. 🧠 Learn how to build your next AI application with practical resources and expert guidance. 🚀 Explore investment opportunities in the future of AI and join our community-backed growth journey. PS: Want to share your AI tool or company news with our 14M+ community? Get in touch.\nKeep reading on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/how-did-anthropics-ai-find-10000-software-security-issues-v9tte?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BiX0Lk1YFTbS0N%2BoVEz4jcA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=2nhsm8PbQYDcg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_-null-icq8ji~mplku1h6~fs-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mplku1h6-fs\n  \n      \n\n----------------------------------------\n\nThis email was intended for Yashica Lohar (Flipkart GWC 7.0 Scholar | CSE ’28 @ IIIT Jabalpur | 2 ⭐ @Codechef)\nLearn why we included this: https://www.linkedin.com/help/linkedin/answer/4788?lang=en&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BiX0Lk1YFTbS0N%2BoVEz4jcA