midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=17MGLI5ZnOFYg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_-null-icq8ji~mpok024m~a3-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mpok024m-a3\n  \nWelcome back! This week is about one clear shift: AI teams are becoming more efficient, more structured, and more agentic. Claude’s prompt caching shows how developers can save hundreds of millions of tokens. Code with Claude London shows why AI learning needs a clear path, not just a playlist. Anthropic’s team shares practical prompting lessons from real debugging. And Google’s agent skills repo shows how agents can get the right knowledge at the right time. In today\'s Atlas Newsletter: Prompt caching in Claude: how one feature saved 300 million tokens in a week without changing a single setting? Code with Claude London: the 24-video playlist mapped into a learning path from model basics to enterprise scale. Anthropic\'s prompting playbook: six lessons from a live debugging session that beats most paid courses. Google\'s agent skills repo: why the next wave of AI performance may come from context architecture rather than bigger models? PS: Want to share your AI tool or company news with our 14M+ community? Get in touch. How to save millions of Claude Tokens in 10 minutes? Claude has a feature that can save developers millions of tokens a week. One creator reportedly saved 91 million tokens in a single day and more than 300 million in a week without changing a single setting. That is prompt caching working as intended. The idea is simple: Claude does not need to reprocess the same context every time. Cached tokens cost around 10% of normal input tokens. So if Claude reads 91 million cached tokens, the effective cost is closer to 9 million. That is the difference between burning context repeatedly and reusing it intelligently. Claude’s cache is organized in layers: System layer: base instructions, tools, and output style. Project layer: CLAUDE.md, memory, and project rules. Conversation layer: messages and replies rebuilt each turn. The problem is that many users accidentally break the cache. → Switch models mid-session? Cache resets. → Let a session sit idle for more than an hour? Cache goes cold. → Use sub-agents heavily? Their cache window is much shorter. → Run /compact or /clear? You reset the cache. This matters because high cache hit rates reduce cost, improve speed, and make session limits feel much more generous. Want the three habits that help most users save tokens and keep Claude sessions efficient? Subscribe here. Don’t miss it From data storytelling to enterprise AI strategy Data that doesn\'t drive decisions. AI pilots that drain the budget. Strategy documents nobody acts on. Losing the room when somebody pushes back. These four GenAI Academy courses help business professionals turn AI from a talking point into real capability. Special offers available.  Data Storytelling with AI → Turn messy data into persuasive stories that move stakeholders to act.  Enterprise AI Platform Blueprint → Build the AI platform roadmap your board will fund and approve.  AI-Powered Business Strategy → Build a 90-day AI adoption plan tailored to our role and team. High-Stakes Leadership Conversations → Simulate the leadership conversations that make or break your credibility. A complete roadmap for learning Claude Code You saved the YouTube playlist for Code with Claude London 2026. But how many videos have you actually finished? And more importantly: Did you know which video to start with? That is not a motivation problem. It is a navigation problem. The playlist gives you 24 videos, but no clear map. It becomes much easier to follow when the sessions are organized into one learning workflow: Vision → Model choice → Prompting → Claude Code → Agents → Production → Engineering organization → Enterprise scale. And here is the roadmap: Start with the keynote and capability sessions to understand where Claude is going. Move into model selection, reasoning control, and prompting to build the foundation. Go deeper into Claude Code and proactive agent workflows. Once you understand the tooling, move into production agents, managed agents, and prompt-to-production workflows. Study how AI-native engineering teams scale these practices across organizations. The playlist is a roadmap for learning how Claude moves from model, to coding assistant, to agent, to operating system for engineering teams. Do you want to learn AI from basics to advanced level and use it at work? Feel free to reach out: https://academy.genai.works/ Why does Anthropic\'s 33-minute prompting playbook beat most paid courses? At Code with Claude, Anthropic applied AI engineer Margot Vanlar walked through a live prompt debugging session. The example was simple: A customer support bot was failing its evals. Instead of adding random instructions, the team fixed it by targeting one failure mode at a time. Six lessons stood out. First, structure before you debug. The bot had role, reasoning instructions, tone, and policy crammed into one paragraph. Separating them with XML tags improved eval scores before any targeted fix. Second, build evals first. The team tested a control case, a proration calculation, a legacy plan question, a billing escalation case, and an information-withholding scenario before changing the prompt. Without evals, you are only guessing. Third, audit your legacy patches. Old instructions added to fix older model behavior can overfit newer models. One patch made the bot refuse to share a customer’s own hotspot allowance, even when the account context had the answer. Fourth, instructions do not create capability. If the task requires reliable calculation, give the model a tool. Telling it to “always calculate correctly” did not fix billing math. Adding a proration tool did. Fifth, state both sides of a trade-off. The bot was told escalation costs $8 and hurts resolution metrics. So it stopped escalating. Adding the cost of not escalating, including refunds and damaged trust, corrected the behavior. Sixth, split complex tasks into separate prompts. A scheduling task failed as one large prompt, even with stronger models. A generate-evaluate-repair loop passed all five test cases with fewer tokens and lower latency. What methods do you use to correct and improve the output of AI tools when they fail? Share your ideas. Google launches an open-source skills repo for AI agents 🤖  Feeding an AI agent good information has always been harder than it looks. Pasting entire documentation sites into the context window creates bloat, drives up token costs, and confuses models that have to sort relevant from irrelevant information on the fly. Google’s new open-source skills repo reframes how agents handle knowledge. It takes a different approach: compact Markdown files, organized by topic, that agents load on demand. Coverage includes: Agent Platform APIs: Gemini API, Gemini Interactions API, Managed Agents API, and Skill Registry API. GCP Basics: AlloyDB, BigQuery, Cloud Run, Cloud SQL, Firebase, and GKE. Well-Architected framework: Security, Reliability, Cost Optimization, Operational Excellence, Performance Optimization, and Sustainability. Recipes: Google Cloud Onboarding, Authentication, and Network Observability.  The install path is a single command: npx skills add google/skills From there, agents pull in only the skills relevant to the task at hand instead of ingesting a full documentation library upfront. The repo is still early. Google is actively soliciting bug reports, skill suggestions, and feature requests from the developer community, which suggests the current coverage is a starting point rather than a finished product. What makes it worth watching is the underlying design assumption: The next wave of agent performance gains may come from cleaner context architecture, not just more capable models. In other words, better agents will depend on how intelligently we feed the model the right knowledge at the right time. What do you think? Will better agents come from bigger models, or from smarter context architecture? Everything else you shouldn’t miss: BadHost: the vulnerability hiding in your AI stack: The \"BadHost\" vulnerability in Starlette, the Python framework behind FastAPI and hundreds of millions of weekly downloads, could give attackers unauthorized access to any AI agent using MCP to connect to external databases, credentials or enterprise systems. xAI enters the coding agent race: Grok Build, a new coding agent and CLI now in beta for SuperGrok and X Premium Plus subscribers, takes direct aim at Claude Code with plan mode, headless automation and parallel subagent support. One API for every model\'s specs and pricing: Models.dev is an open-source GitHub repo that consolidates specifications and pricing across AI models into a single queryable API. DeepMind solved decade-old math problems for a few hundred dollars each: AlphaProof Nexus autonomously cracked nine open Erdős problems, including questions that had gone unanswered for decades, at inference costs of a few hundred dollars per solution. Learn more about AI from the experts building it 📸 Follow us on Instagram for fast, visual AI updates in 30 seconds.  🐝 Subscribe to our Generative AI daily newsletter — trusted by 120 000+ subscribers — to stay ahead of the latest AI news across tech, education, and business.  📺 Watch us on YouTube to hear insights directly from leading AI voices, builders, and innovators. 🐦 Follow us on X for breaking AI news and real-time industry updates. 🧠 Learn how to build your next AI application with practical resources and expert guidance. 🚀 Explore investment opportunities in the future of AI and join our community-backed growth journey. PS: Want to share your AI tool or company news with our 14M+ community? Get in touch.\nKeep reading on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/how-can-you-