Gary Vaynerchuk\n\n\nBy Gary Vaynerchuk\n\n    \n    \n    Read this article on LinkedIn to join the conversation\nRead on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/new-york-knicks-town-gary-vaynerchuk-lgc7e?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BRYSBRsiDQkGr87r7szPBRA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=0G9KelydljLIg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_-null-icq8ji~mpu8n5p1~rn-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mpu8n5p1-rn\n  \nI’m a diehard Knicks fan. I’m not joking. Sports is my great outlet, my pressure valve, and my greatest source of irrational joy. Right now, I am feeling a lot of things, and most of them are loud. When I tell you that the New York Knicks swept the Cleveland Cavaliers in the 2026 Eastern Conference Finals to reach the NBA Finals for the first time since 1999, I need you to understand what that means. This is a release of tension that has been building for over a quarter-century. Twenty-seven years. Let that sink in. I’m stunned… I don’t know how to act. I don’t know who to call. Do I dap up the guy at the bodega? Do I call my brother and scream? Right now, life is good. Life is real good. New York is vibrant in a way it only gets when the orange and blue are the last team standing in the East. This Feels Like ‘99 This is crazy how much this feels like ‘99, but with a different kind of dominance. In 1999, we were a scrappy eight seed. We were the underdogs who stunned the top-seeded Heat with an Allan Houston flopper and then swept the Hawks in round two. I still remember that series vividly. Every time I see Steve Smith anywhere, I smile because I’m like, “Yeah, we f*cked you guys up in that four-game sweep.” But 1999 was also defined by heartbreak. Patrick Ewing got hurt, and without him, we had to face the “Twin Towers” of San Antonio, Tim Duncan and David Robinson. Duncan was just unstoppable. We lost in five. That was the last time we tasted the Finals. Since then, it’s been a desert. We’ve had a few oases—Linsanity, the 2012-13 Melo year, the first Randle playoff run—but nothing like this. The kids don’t know the history, and that’s a problem. They don’t know the pain of being the first eight seed in history to make the Finals only to have the engine fall out. They don’t know the “what ifs.” But this 2026 team? They aren’t an eight seed. They are a juggernaut. They just won 11 games in a row in the playoffs, all by double digits. They aren’t sneaking into the Finals; they are breaking the door down. Why This Means So Much I’m a kid that came to America young, landed in Queens, then Jersey, and sports became a big part of my life from the beginning. Specifically, the Jets and Knicks. If you know New York in the ‘80s and ‘90s, you know exactly what I mean…the toughness, the grind, the swagger. I’ve invested emotional energy into this team for the past 40 years. And the losing became part of the fun. The adversity became the fun. Being a big Knick fan has usually been about waking up the next morning after devastating losses and still talking yourself into next season. It’s like life, man. Because you wanna f*cking win in life? Fall in love with losing, because most people are scared of it. There’s nothing to fear. The Architecture of the 2026 Run Jalen Brunson: The New King of New York Brunson is well on his way to his own Mount Rushmore. We were starving for a point guard who could dictate the terms of a game for 20 years. What Jalen has done since coming from Dallas is nothing short of miraculous. Think about the legacy of Clyde Frazier and Willis Reed. Those guys are the gods of this franchise. Then you have Ewing. But Brunson? If he brings a title to 33rd Street, the conversation changes. He is the first domino that didn’t just fall…he pushed the rest of the league out of the way. He’s out there ISO-ing the best defenders in the world and making them look like they’re wearing concrete boots. The “Magic” of KAT The reason we’ve won so many in a row is that we’re running the offense through Karl-Anthony Towns (KAT) in a way nobody expected. His dad’s favorite player was Magic Johnson, and you can see it in his game right now. He’s happy facilitating. He’s a big boy who can pass over the top of the defense, and when you have a 7-footer playing like a point-hub, it creates too much size and too much gravity for teams like Cleveland or Atlanta to handle. The Grinders: Bridges and Deuce You can’t win in New York without grit. Mikal Bridges was worth every single draft pick. He woke up in the Philly series and hasn’t looked back. And Deuce McBride? He’s the unsung hero. He was hurt for a stretch, but he came back and started hitting those back-breaking threes. He’s the embodiment of the “next man up” mentality that defines a championship roster. The Collector Mentality and the Madness of Knicks Culture Want to know how die-hard I actually am? I’m flying to Game 1 in San Antonio on Wednesday. I own a lot of collectibles from this time. Brunson, Hart, Karl-Anthony Towns. I’ve been buying up OG Anunoby stuff too. Because if this team wins... I mean we’re still talking about a dunk John Starks made 30 years ago and we didn’t even win a title. If this team wins a title, all of the collectibles around it go through the roof. But it’s not even about the money. It’s about what happens to New York when the Knicks matter. There’s just something different that happens when the Knicks make a run, and it’s obviously enthralling to everyone who’s actually a die-hard. You feel it walking into delis, you hear it from cab drivers, you see it in people’s faces at the bodega. The city gets lighter and louder, more connected. That’s why this run feels so emotional to people who’ve been carrying this team around in their hearts for decades. Why I Love Sports At the end of the day, this is why I love sports. You can’t cheat in sports. You either won the game or you didn’t. There are no “participation trophies” in the NBA Finals. It’s the closest thing to business, either you build something that works or you don’t. You either get the sponsorship or you don’t. When you go into that arena, or into a UFC cage, it’s the realest version of life. And right now, the reality for New York is that we are the best in the East. Four more wins. That’s it. Let’s go.\nKeep reading on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/new-york-knicks-town-gary-vaynerchuk-lgc7e?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BRYSBRsiDQkGr87r7szPBRA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=0G9KelydljLIg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_-null-icq8ji~mpu8n5p1~rn-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mpu8n5p1-rn\n  \n      \n\n----------------------------------------\n\nThis email was intended for Yashica Lohar (Flipkart GWC 7.0 Scholar | CSE ’28 @ IIIT Jabalpur | 2 ⭐ @Codechef)\nLearn why we included this: https://www.linkedin.com/help/linkedin/answer/4788?lang=en&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BRYSBRsiDQkGr87r7szPBRA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=0G9KelydljLIg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer-null-icq8ji~mpu8n5p1~rn-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mpu8n5p1-rn\nYou are receiving LinkedIn notification emails. Others can see that you are a subscriber.\n\n\nUnsubscribe: https://www.linkedin.com/series-notifications/?action=unsubscribe&memberToken=ADoAAEIlwb4B7wSObgzu320aKxhPrfHhU2UtxwY&newsletterId=6641420720876343296&newsletterTitle=LinkedIn 360 l By GaryVee&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BRYSBRsiDQkGr87r7szPBRA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=0G9KelydljLIg1&ek=email_series_follow_newsletter_02&e=icq8ji-mpu8n5