TcSjx3spYYKGBA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=2jl59yZyX6Ssg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_-null-icq8ji~mq17fg3c~ls-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mq17fg3c-ls\n  \nA lot of marketers still don’t understand that making content through affiliates in a TikTok environment and standing up the production of a live show that sells is mission-critical to a consumer brand in 2026. Many of them still think it’s a nice-to-have. They’ll get to it when it matters. It’s been mattering. It really matters. Just like social media. Just like mobile. Just like everything that has ever come along that mattered. Big companies will be three to five years away and then they’ll overspend on acquisitions to close the gap. One of the pillars that I think is most important in contemporary marketing and business is attention. If you can boil it down for anyone who’s trying to sell a product or service, it’s all about attention. Everything else is distribution. If you don’t get those two things right, you’re left in the dust. I think a lot of marketers don’t actually know how profound the amount of attention being allocated by the human race to social networks really is. When you have TikTok using its UI and UX to help billions of users discover live shopping channels in real time through the For You Page, you’re talking about one of the great opportunities of all time. The QVC Lesson Everyone Forgot I do not think people understand how high the stakes are. People love to dismiss live shopping as some new trend…yet what’s fascinating is that QVC succeeded despite all the friction. You have to turn on the television. You have to find the channel. You have to watch at the right time. Compared to grabbing your phone and being in your normal environment, it’s incredibly inconvenient. The brilliance of QVC is that the format works. Humans buy from humans. The entertainment works. Ask yourself a simple question: are you more likely to buy something from a human being who is live, explaining the product, answering questions, demonstrating it in real time, or from a static image that shows up in your feed? The answer is obvious. Yet somehow people continue to underestimate the power of the format. Commerce Becomes Community One of the great things about social selling is that it isn’t just selling. When you watch QVC, somebody is selling to you. When you buy on TikTok or other social commerce platforms, there’s a community watching together. You’re entertaining each other. You’re reacting together. You’re talking to one another. That level of communal commerce is profound. People become friends in these rooms. People who consistently watch the same shows start forming relationships with each other. That’s a very different experience from traditional retail. Most people aren’t becoming close friends with strangers in a shoe store. On a live shopping show, everyone’s interacting with everyone. The commerce is important. The community is what makes it stick. This Isn’t Coming. It’s Already Here There is zero doubt in my mind that we’ll look back at conversations like this in nine years and say, “We told them.” We’re not geniuses. We’re from the future. This is happening every day. There are millions and millions of dollars being sold through live shopping in America right now. We don’t even have to point to China anymore. It’s already happening here. Meta is going to do it. YouTube is going to do it. Snapchat will do it. Twitch is already experimenting with it. Every platform that captures attention will eventually move toward commerce because the economics are too attractive. I don’t see how people miss this. It’s happening in plain sight. Live Shopping Is Bigger Than Products Everyone thinks live commerce is about selling hats, collectibles, or random gadgets. That’s because they’re looking at where the market starts instead of where it ends. This is going to be about selling homes. It’s going to be about selling cars. It’s going to be about selling services. As a public speaker, I know that if you put me on camera and allow me to auction or sell an hour of my time live, I’ll do a much better job than a static listing on a website ever could. Human interaction sells. Storytelling sells. Demonstration sells. The same principle applies to almost every category you can imagine. The Future Isn’t AI or Live Shopping. It’s Both A lot of people think AI will eliminate shopping experiences. I actually think it creates a more interesting split. There are plenty of things I don’t care about. I’d happily let an AI agent reorder my deodorant, household goods, batteries, or anything else I buy repeatedly. I want convenience. But for the things I do care about, live shopping becomes even more important. Imagine you’ve automated your purchases, but then you stumble across a founder on a live stream. They’re entertaining, they’re authentic, and you like their energy. They introduce a product you never knew existed. Suddenly, you’re reconsidering your default choice. That’s where live commerce wins. Live commerce creates discovery, creates relevance, and creates new consideration. It’s how brands break through when everything else becomes automated. The products people care about will still be sold through human connection. The Big Opportunity Is for Ordinary People The part of this that excites me most isn’t the creators making millions of dollars. That will happen. There will absolutely be people making a million dollars a year sitting in front of a camera selling products. What excites me more is the person who makes an extra $9,000. Extra income might mean a family can finally take a vacation. It might mean paying off debt. It might mean reducing stress. It might mean creating options. When I wrote Crush It! in 2009 and talked about people making money online, many people laughed. Today, almost every teenager wants to become a creator. What I think people still underestimate is that being a great social seller is a different skill from being a content creator. There are a lot of people whose greatest skill is simply the gift of gab. They’re charismatic. They’re persuasive, and they know how to connect with people. In the past, they might have worked in a department store selling clothes. In the future, they’ll be online selling products, earning commissions, building audiences, and creating income streams for themselves. Instead of television networks collecting all the money from advertising, those dollars will increasingly flow to millions of individuals. That’s the part I find most exciting. My Advice: Give It 20 Hours My number one piece of advice is simple: try. Please don’t sit in the crowd and be cynical. Please don’t immediately decide that live social commerce won’t work, that it’s not for you, or that it’s all hype. Before you say no, say maybe. Spend 10 hours studying, watch people who are succeeding, learn the mechanics, and understand how it works. Then spend 10 hours actually doing it. I’m asking for 20 hours. Twenty hours that could potentially change your life. If you invest those 20 hours, you’ll understand what we’re talking about. You’ll get enough exposure to decide whether this is for you or not. The feedback loop is immediate. You’ll learn quickly, and at the very least, you’ll understand one of the most important things happening in business today.\nKeep reading on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/live-commerce-biggest-opportunity-since-early-gary-vaynerchuk-oduwe?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BrbEtUCl5TcSjx3spYYKGBA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=2jl59yZyX6Ssg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_-null-icq8ji~mq17fg3c~ls-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mq17fg3c-ls\n  \n      \n\n----------------------------------------\n\nThis email was intended for Yashica Lohar (Flipkart GWC 7.0 Scholar | CSE ’28 @ IIIT Jabalpur | 2 ⭐ @Codechef)\nLearn why we included this: https://www.linkedin.com/help/linkedin/answer/4788?lang=en&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BrbEtUCl5TcSjx3spYYKGBA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=2jl59yZyX6Ssg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer-null-icq8ji~mq17fg3c~ls-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mq17fg3c-ls\nYou are receiving LinkedIn notification emails. Others can see that you are a subscriber.\n\n\nUnsubscribe: https://www.linkedin.com/series-notifications/?action=unsubscribe&memberToken=ADoAAEIlwb4B7wSObgzu320aKxhPrfHhU2UtxwY&newsletterId=6641420720876343296&newsletterTitle=LinkedIn 360 l By GaryVee&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BrbEtUCl5TcSjx3spYYKGBA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=2jl59yZyX6Ssg1&ek=email_series_follow_newsletter_02&e=icq8ji-mq17fg3c-ls&eid=icq8ji-mq17fg3c-ls&m=unsubscribe&ts=footerGlimmer&li=0&t=plh    ·  Help: https://www.linkedin.com/help/linkedin/answer/67?lang=en&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BrbEtUCl5TcSjx3spYYKGBA%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=2jl59yZyX6Ssg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-help-0-textfooterglimmer&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-help-0-textfooterglimmer-null-icq8ji~mq17fg3c~ls-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mq17fg3c-ls\n\n© 2026 LinkedIn Corporation, 1zwnj000 West Maude Avenue, Sunnyvale, CA 94085.\nLinkedIn and the LinkedIn logo are registered trademarks of LinkedIn.','incoming-complaint','INCOMING',NULL,'2026-06-21 16:52:16',NULL,NULL,NULL),('ae5f0394-6633-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7','d69e6b3d-662d-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Dear LinkedIn 360 l By GaryVee,\n\nWe have received your ticket regarding: \"Live Commerce Is the Biggest Opportunity Since the Early Internet\".\n\nYour reference ID is #d69e6b3d. This issue has been logged and is currently under review by our team.','approved-draft-placeholder','INCOMING',NULL,'2026-06-21 16:52:16',NULL,NULL,NULL),('ae68260a-6633-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7','d69e6b3d-662d-11f1-bb83-601031c340f7',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'The Atlas\n\nBy GenAI Works\n\n\nBy GenAI Works\n\n    \n    \n    Read this article on LinkedIn to join the conversation\nRead on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/how-does-anthropic-marketing-team-use-claude-cowork-genai-works-im7ic?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3Bjxy%2B7Cs5SFq8RFq%2FYfmAvw%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=0FzpmpWgZ0SIg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_-null-icq8ji~mq1bwwo8~k2-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mq1bwwo8-k2\n  \nWelcome back! This week is about AI moving from answers to execution. It starts with Anthropic using its own agent to run real marketing work across data, docs, schedules, Slack, Asana and slides. Then Google enters with a model that could make enterprise AI dramatically cheaper to operate. Next, OpenAI makes four moves to push frontier AI deeper into the systems companies already trust. And Reddit becomes one of the most important surfaces in AI search, shaping what ChatGPT, Claude, Perplexity and Google AI Overviews choose to cite.The takeaway from the four stories is simple. The model alone no longer decides the winner. What decides it is where AI runs, what it costs, which systems it connects to and which sources it trusts. In today\'s Atlas Newsletter: The vendor turned the product on itself: How Anthropic\'s marketing team runs a full weekly business review on Cowork, one operator, one morning. The AI bill that scales to zero: Google\'s Gemma 4 12B runs on your laptop and rewrites the unit economics of frontier AI. OpenAI answered one question four times: AWS, Codex, a life sciences model and a safety play, all pointed at enterprise distribution. Reddit is the page everyone now optimizes for: Why 40% of AI citations trace back to Reddit, and the playbook to get your brand in those threads. PS: Want to share your AI tool or company news with our 14M+ community? Get in touch. How does Anthropic turn Claude Cowork into a marketing operator? Every Sunday at 5 PM, a scheduled task fires. Cowork reads the data warehouse through BigQuery, pulls files from Google Drive, builds the table structure, and writes the prep log. By Monday, the operator opens the app to a working draft with findings already surfaced: A $612M pipeline at 84% to goal. An executive engagement gap in one segment. A reach step-up from spring events. Then one person drives the review. They pick the focus and hand Cowork the sales QBR doc to reconcile against. The agent catches a mismatch before drafting. Then it produces: A v1 review. A proofread version. A branded HTML doc. A Slack post. An Asana task with owner and date. A leadership slide. Research to ship deliverables. One operator. One morning. The detail worth stealing is the posture. Cowork asks clarifying questions, flags risks the operator did not raise, and pushes back instead of agreeing by default. When the sales doc introduced a new segment structure, Cowork stopped and asked whether to follow the new version or footnote the old one. That is the difference between a chat window and an agent.  And the loop improves.  After shipping, the operator asks what should change in the underlying skill, and Cowork appends the week\'s learnings to a file that carries into the next run. This is what AI adoption should look like inside companies. Share your own story. Most people think Claude is a chatbot. It\'s three. Claude has three modes that work differently: Chat for thinking, Cowork for documents and projects, and Code for building. Most people only use the first one, then wonder why everyone\'s making such a big deal. The 2-Day Claude AI Mastery Workshop walks through all three live. By Sunday evening you\'ll have set up Skills and Connectors that automate Notion and files, vibe coded a working dashboard, and pulled together a full presentation Claude built end to end. The workshop also covers 10+ AI tools and workflows that pair with Claude. Saturday and Sunday, 10 AM to 7 PM EST. Free for the next 48 hours. Register free GenAI Works built the platform running G42\'s Agentathon Last week G42 launched its Agentathon, and the platform running the entire event is our Hackathon app. The same app Oracle and Red Bull Racing have used for theirs. G42 partnered with GenAI Works to tap our experience in hackathon management and software development. We built the event landing page, provided the Hackathon app and supported the full participant experience start to finish. The app is already pulling in revenue and users.  Now our community can own a piece of it, along with the rest of the product ecosystem we are building. What the numbers look like: Revenue grew 2.4X in less than a year  Tens of thousands of users in the first month  28K participants across 9 events  30% conversion to Academy users from our World Wide Vibes Hackathon  4 hackathons held in the last 12 months HackathonOS is moving fast. The product team is mid-sprint, building toward two major milestones: the live G42 Agentathon and a $50,000 G42 hackathon planned for later this year. The new additions show where the platform is going: An AI Mentor built on participant, team and resource data, designed to handle 90 to 95% of participant questions on its own. A Communications Hub for admin-scheduled channel posts. A unified Schedule view to keep everyone aligned. LinkedIn auto-fill powered by Apify, feeding smarter team matching. Bulk drag-and-drop resource upload with NLP-based metadata pre-population. The roster already speaks for itself.  Sponsors include IBM, Google, AWS, Oracle, Slack and Red Bull Racing. University partners include Queensland University of Technology, University of Technology Sydney, The University of Queensland, RMIT University, La Trobe University and St. Joseph\'s College of Engineering and Technology. HackathonOS is growing into the operating system for running large-scale innovation programs. This can be your success story too. Own an early piece of an already winning product. Join the round. Disclaimer: In making an investment decision, investors must rely on their own examination of the issuer and the terms of the offering, including the merits and risks involved. Genai Works, Inc. has filed a Form C with the Securities and Exchange Commission in connection with its offering, a copy of which may be obtained here Google introduces Gemma 4 12B that runs on your laptop For three years, powerful AI came with a meter running.  You paid per query, per token or per seat. And the cost climbed with every use.  Google and Google DeepMind shipped a model that breaks that arrangement: Gemma 4 12B runs on your laptop.  If your machine has 16GB of memory, you own the compute and the bill stops there. The math gets interesting at scale. A team running 10,000 queries a month on a cloud-hosted model pays indefinitely. A team running the same queries locally pays once, at the point of hardware purchase.  For startups, agencies and enterprise teams watching their AI spend, that\'s a real change in unit economics. The capability is what makes it worth the attention.  Gemma 4 12B reads text, processes images and listens to audio natively.  It\'s the first model of its size to take audio as a primary input, and it benchmarks above models more than twice as large.  Smaller footprint, competitive performance, zero recurring cost. Privacy comes built in. When the model runs locally, your data never leaves your machine.  The open license clears the last barrier.  No vendor lock-in, no usage agreement, no permission required to build on top of it.  People have already shipped wearable robotic arms using earlier models in the same family. What would you build now if the cost of access dropped to zero? OpenAI Introduces new capabilities for their AI models OpenAI made enterprise AI much easier to buy. Four announcements. One direction: Move frontier AI closer to the systems, budgets and teams enterprises already trust. 1. OpenAI is now deeper inside AWS OpenAI frontier models and Codex are now generally available through Amazon Bedrock. That means security review, compliance, billing and procurement can run through AWS instead of a separate OpenAI contract. For companies already on AWS, this removes one of the biggest barriers to production. Commercial and GovCloud regions are supported. Next up, OpenAI says Daybreak, its cyber model for secure code review and patch validation, is coming to AWS customers. 2. Codex is becoming a workplace tool Five million people use Codex weekly. 20% of them are not developers, and that group is growing 3x faster than the developer base. This week, OpenAI shipped six role-specific plugins for: Data analytics. Creative production. Sales. Product design. Public equity investing. Investment banking. Together, they bundle 62 tools and 110 skills. Codex now reaches well beyond coding. 3. GPT-Rosalind is OpenAI\'s vertical play GPT-Rosalind is built for life sciences: Drug discovery. Genomics. Biomedical research. Medicinal chemistry. Experimental design. On OpenAI\'s LifeSciBench, judged by external biology experts, it scored 63% overall. GPT-5.5 scored 59%. Gemini 3.1 Pro scored 51%. Grok 4.3 scored 34%. For pharma and biotech buyers, this is OpenAI moving into specialized scientific work, not another generic model demo. 4. OpenAI is shaping the safety rails Ahead of the G7 meeting in Évian, OpenAI called for an international youth AI safety institute. It also published nine principles covering age verification, parental controls, data privacy, annual risk assessments and a ban on targeted advertising to minors. Read next to the AWS and Codex news, the pattern is clear. OpenAI is selling models and helping define the infrastructure and rules around how enterprises and governments will adopt them. The takeaway The frontier model race is about distribution. Who gets into the systems companies already use? Who fits the procurement process? Who earns trust from security, compliance and finance? This week, OpenAI answered that question four times. Reddit is now the most-cited source in AI search Your brand is invisible in AI search if Reddit is ignoring you. Here\'s the uncomfortable part: Reddit is becoming the new SEO layer. Reddit now accounts for 40% of AI citations across ChatGPT, Perplexity and Claude.  Not polished blogs or landing pages or case studies. Reddit threads. Search \"best CRM for startups\" on Google.  In 2023 Reddit was barely there.  Now Reddit threads often sit on page 1, sometimes above G2, Capterra and the SaaS companies themselves.  Ask ChatGPT the same question and it pulls from Reddit too. That means people are discovering brands through the conversations AI trusts, not just through search. Here\'s the playbook: Find the Reddit threads ranking for your keywords. Search your main terms on Google and note which subreddits keep appearing. Answer like a real user. Helpful replies win. Promotional replies get ignored. Show up in \"best X for Y\" and \"X vs Y\" threads. AI leans on comparison threads because buyers do. Monitor what people say about your brand. Positive mentions today become AI recommendations tomorrow. Keep contributing. Reddit visibility moves fast, so one good comment is not a strategy. Never spam. There\'s already an industry selling aged accounts, paid upvotes and fake AEO mentions. It works for now. Bought citations end the way bought backlinks did: filtered, penalized, forgotten. The boring strategy survives.  Show up where your customers already talk, answer honestly, add value and earn the mention. Google ranks Reddit threads. AI cites Reddit threads.  Your brand needs to be in those threads before your competitors become the answer. Act now. Everything else you shouldn’t miss: Washington wants a cut of the AI boom: Senior US officials have floated taking government equity stakes in major AI companies, an idea Sam Altman reportedly pitched last year. Picture Uncle Sam on the cap table, sharing the upside but also riding the market\'s stomach-drops. The $1.78 trillion IPO that locks Europe out: SpaceX is readying the biggest IPO ever, up to $86B at a $1.78T valuation, with Anthropic and OpenAI right behind it. The fees funnel to a tiny club of US banks while European lenders watch from the cheap seats. One in seven Canadians now confess money problems to a chatbot: 15% of Canadians asked AI for financial guidance last year, many because they did not want to feel judged. The friendly tone is the draw and the danger: ask it whether to start a business and it cheerleads past the risk. The AI bubble talk just cost Seoul 5.5% in a day: South Korea\'s Kospi cratered as much as 7% after Broadcom\'s revenue forecast missed and spooked the AI rally. One chip giant\'s guidance, a continent of red. Barclays says the AI rally is running too hot: Barclays is flagging the AI trade as stretched, with investor euphoria and rising rate risk building underneath valuations that have climbed all year. When the bank financing the boom starts hedging out loud, the room hears it. SoftBank commits $87B to France\'s biggest-ever AI data center: SoftBank is putting up to $87B behind what would become the largest AI data center project in France, on top of a $60B-plus OpenAI stake. The infrastructure spree keeps accelerating while everyone else debates whether the bubble talk is real. Learn more about AI from the experts building it 📸 Follow us on Instagram for fast, visual AI updates in 30 seconds.  🐝 Subscribe to our Generative AI daily newsletter — trusted by 120 000+ subscribers — to stay ahead of the latest AI news across tech, education, and business.  📺 Watch us on YouTube to hear insights directly from leading AI voices, builders, and innovators. 🐦 Follow us on X for breaking AI news and real-time industry updates. 🧠 Learn how to build your next AI application with practical resources and expert guidance. 🚀 Explore investment opportunities in the future of AI and join our community-backed growth journey. PS: Want to collaborate with the biggest GTM engine and distribution moat for AI companies? You can feature your tool with us and reach millions.\nKeep reading on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/how-does-anthropic-marketing-team-use-claude-cowork-genai-works-im7ic?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3Bjxy%2B7Cs5SFq8RFq%2FYfmAvw%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=0FzpmpWgZ0SIg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_-null-icq8ji~mq1bwwo8~k2-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mq1bwwo8-k2\n  \n      \n\n----------------------------------------\n\nThis email was intended for Yashica Lohar (Flipkart GWC 7.0 Scholar | CSE ’28 @ IIIT Jabalpur | 2 ⭐ @Codechef)\nLearn why we included this: https://www.linkedin.com/help/linkedin/answer/4788?lang=en&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3Bjxy%2B7Cs5SFq8RFq%2FYfmAvw%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=0FzpmpWgZ0SIg1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer-null-icq8ji~mq1bwwo8~k2-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mq1bwwo8-k2\nYou are receiving LinkedIn notification emails. Others can see that you are a subscriber.\n\n\nUnsubscribe: https://www.linkedin.com/series-notifications/?action=unsubscribe&memberToken=ADoAAEIlwb4B7wSObgzu320aKxhPrfHhU2UtxwY&newsletterId=7075090904906149891&newsletterTitle=The Atlas&