S,\n\nWe have received your ticket regarding: \"[JOIN NOW] We are live streaming the resume optimization workshop right now\".\n\nYour reference ID is #e723c4e3. This issue has been logged and is currently under review by our team.','approved-draft-placeholder','INCOMING',NULL,'2026-06-21 16:52:16',NULL,NULL,NULL),('e730fddf-6703-11f1-a09a-8107a00d61c0','e72ffad3-6703-11f1-a09a-8107a00d61c0',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'The Atlas\n\nBy GenAI Works\n\n\nBy GenAI Works\n\n    \n    \n    Read this article on LinkedIn to join the conversation\nRead on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/claudes-fable-5-took-crypto-test-genai-works-r6fsf?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BFiVqBTRTTpm%2BFpDHbqn2dg%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=3BZhvW8Ag30sk1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-null-0-read_more_banner_cta_-null-icq8ji~mqb6c5fn~7c-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mqb6c5fn-7c\n  \nWelcome back! Anthropic\'s Fable 5 is having a week. It topped a major agent leaderboard by the widest margin the board has ever seen, then got tested on a crypto trading desk to see whether capability translates into prediction. The result is a useful reminder: AI is often better at identifying what matters than telling you exactly what happens next. That lesson runs through today\'s stories. Fable 5 leads the field in coding and agentic tasks yet still misses the numbers that determine a trade. ChatGPT is getting a memory that updates itself, right up until users want to understand or control what it remembers. Google just made local AI up to 4x faster with DiffusionGemma, but only if the hardware can keep up. The models keep getting smarter. The real challenge is learning when to trust them, when to verify them, and when to stay in the loop. In today\'s Atlas Newsletter: Fable 5 on the trading desk: the first serious test of Claude on Bitcoin, Ethereum and XRP, and where it helps traders versus where it falls short. Fable 5 takes #1 on Agent Arena: Anthropic\'s new model tops the leaderboard by a wide margin, with one category where Opus still wins. OpenAI taught ChatGPT to dream: Dreaming V3 rebuilds memory to update itself in the background, and you can audit what it knows. Google\'s DiffusionGemma: an open model that drafts whole blocks of text at once for up to 4x faster local generation. PS: Want to collaborate with the biggest GTM engine and distribution moat for AI companies? You can feature your tool with us and reach millions. How useful is Claude\'s Fable 5 model for crypto traders?  Claude was asked to predict Bitcoin. It got the story right. It got the numbers wrong. The first serious test of Fable 5 on crypto is in. And the results reveal exactly where AI helps traders and where it still falls short.  BeInCrypto tested Fable 5 on Bitcoin, Ethereum, and XRP.  The model had to identify the most important metric for each asset, estimate a price range, define a downside floor, and explain what could prove its thesis wrong.  Then every prediction was checked against real market data. The pattern was remarkably consistent. Across all three assets, Fable correctly identified the key metric to watch and the direction of the market. What it struggled with was timing and magnitude. Bitcoin: right thesis, wrong numbers Fable focused on long-term holders and correctly identified the shift from selling to accumulation. However, it dated the turning point incorrectly and underestimated Bitcoin fund outflows by a factor of six.  Ethereum: right signal, incomplete picture  The model highlighted the staking queue as a bullish indicator and got that call right.  What it missed was the scale of ETF outflows that continued to put pressure on the market. XRP: the strongest performance  Fable framed XRP as a battle between new token supply and institutional demand.  The demand side played out almost exactly as expected. Yet once again, the model significantly overestimated the size of the supply increase.  So how useful is Fable 5 for traders? Useful for getting oriented. Not for sizing a trade.  It reads the right metric and the right direction, then misses the numbers that decide your position.  Use it to figure out what to watch, then pull the real data yourself before any money moves. How are you using AI in your own trading research right now? Share your thoughts. At the Oracle AI World Tour 2026 in New York, Oracle introduced 25 autonomous agentic applications across ERP, supply chain, HR, sales, service and marketing. All of them were built in Oracle AI Agent Studio. All of them target work that enterprise teams are still doing manually today. Finance and supply chain received 12 agents, including a Collectors Workspace that helps manage cash flow and a Logistics Execution Command Center that connects transportation and warehouse operations in one place. HR received 8 agents, including a Manager Concierge that supports compensation, performance and talent decisions. Sales, service and marketing received 5 agents, including a Marketing Command Center that turns scattered customer data into coordinated growth execution. The important part is that these agents run inside the organization\'s existing security framework. They operate within existing controls, permissions and guardrails rather than creating a separate AI layer outside the enterprise environment. That matters because many companies want agentic AI without new security risks, data exposure or uncontrolled automation. Oracle\'s bet is that AI agents will be adopted faster when they live inside the systems companies already trust. The direction is clear. Enterprise AI is moving from pilots to embedded execution, with specialized agents sitting inside finance, HR, operations and customer-facing teams. The question for business leaders is simple: Which manual process would you hand to an enterprise AI agent first? We broke down what this shift means for teams putting agents to work.  The full take is worth a look: https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:ugcPost:7470816847366594561/ Don\'t miss it. GenAI Works\' next stage: from AI content leader to product ecosystem. We started by helping people learn about AI. Two years on, that grew into a 14M+ community, 300+ paying customers and 2.4x revenue in 2024-2025. Now we\'re entering the next stage, moving from sharing AI to teaching it so people can apply it in their work. We do that through our ecosystem of products, and they\'re already gaining traction. AI Academy launched with 30K students and $12K in month one. ToneUp and the Hackathon app come next. To fund this stage, we opened a crowdfunding round. Join from $1,000. Up to 25% bonus shares before July 1, 2026. Own part of GenAI products today In making an investment decision, investors must rely on their own examination of the issuer and the terms of the offering, including the merits and risks involved. Genai Works, Inc. has filed a Form C with the Securities and Exchange Commission in connection with its offering, a copy of which may be obtained here: https://bit.ly/3APlUkJ Claude Fable 5 ranks #1 on the new Agent Arena leaderboard Claude Fable 5 just took #1 on Agent Arena, and the margin is the story. On the Agent board it scores 12.94%, ahead of GPT 5.5 xHigh at 10.56% and Claude Opus 4.8 Thinking at 9.26%.  For a leaderboard where the top models usually cluster within a point or two of each other, that spread is wide. Agent Arena tests models on millions of real long-horizon tasks using web search, filesystem and terminal tools. The actual work people do: writing code, building slide decks, researching, shipping apps, analyzing documents. Fable 5 came out on top. Where did it dominate? WebDev: First place at 1665, more than 90 points clear of the next model at 1566. On a board where rivals sit a handful of points apart, that gap is enormous. Text: First overall at 1510, leading a top ten that Anthropic models almost entirely own. Category sweep: Across the breakdown of Overall, Hard Prompts, Coding, Creative Writing, Instruction Following and Longer Query, Fable 5 lands first in nearly every column. Where did it slip? Vision: Second at 1307, just behind Opus 4.7 Thinking. Document: Fifth at 1495, behind four other Anthropic models. So the picture is a model that is exceptional at building and writing and merely strong at reading images and parsing documents.  When Fable 5 is suited to the task, it pulls away from the field.  In the few categories where it isn\'t the best Anthropic option, an Opus model still edges it out. What makes Arena worth watching is that it grades on real outcomes from tasks submitted by users worldwide, not synthetic benchmarks a model can be tuned to game.  Coming out on top there means the wins showed up in actual work, not a test set. The takeaway for builders:  If your job is front-end code, app generation or long-form text, Fable 5 is now the model to beat by a clear margin. If your work leans heavily on documents or vision, it\'s worth checking whether an Opus model fits better before you switch. Congrats to Anthropic on the milestone 👏 OpenAI taught ChatGPT to dream  OpenAI is rolling out Dreaming V3, the biggest rebuild of ChatGPT memory since the feature launched in April 2024.  Plus and Pro users in the US get it first, with Free and Go users and more countries following over the coming weeks. Here is what changed under the hood. The original 2024 memory, now called saved memories, only wrote down what you explicitly told it to remember.  Ask it to note your July trip to Singapore and it kept that note. Everything else evaporated.  Those saved notes also decayed, turning stale or flat wrong as your life moved on. In April 2025 OpenAI added the first version of dreaming, a background process that reads across your chat history and curates memory automatically instead of waiting for you to say \"remember this.\"  It supplemented saved memories but never stood on its own. Dreaming V3 is now the full architecture.  It runs in the background to synthesize your memory state across many conversations, optimizing for three things OpenAI uses to grade memory: carrying useful context forward, following your stated preferences and constraints, and staying current as time passes.  That last one matters more than it sounds.  A memory that reads \"planning their birthday party for next Saturday\" has to know when Sunday arrives. OpenAI claims V3 is both more capable and more compute-efficient than the V0 dreaming layer it replaces, which is how they justify running it across hundreds of millions of users over multi-year time horizons. You can audit what it knows.  The memory summary page shows the highlights of what ChatGPT has synthesized about you, and you can add facts, correct them or tell the model which topics to raise and when. Open your memory summary page and see what ChatGPT has quietly pieced together about you. It might know more than you expect 🙂 Google introduces DiffusionGemma 4x faster text generation Owning a local GPU right now is a Ferrari in a school zone. Why? Because most language models act like a typewriter, generating one token at a time from left to right. In the cloud that\'s efficient, because servers batch thousands of requests together to share the hardware load. Run it locally for a single user and the same process leaves your GPU underutilized, spending most of its time waiting for the next keystroke. To open the highway, Google released DiffusionGemma. It is a 26B Mixture of Experts model, activating only 3.8B parameters during inference, that fits inside 18GB of VRAM when quantized and ships under an Apache 2.0 license. Built on the Gemma 4 family and Gemini Diffusion research, it adds a novel diffusion head designed to push generation speed. Here is the shift.  Instead of predicting words one after another, it drafts an entire 256-token block in a single pass, with every token able to attend to all the others at once. That moves the bottleneck from memory bandwidth to compute, which is exactly the resource your local GPU has sitting idle. The result is 1000+ tokens per second on a single NVIDIA H100 and 700+ on a consumer RTX 5090. Think less single typewriter, more printing press stamping the whole block at once. How text diffusion works, in three steps: The canvas: The model starts with a block of random placeholder tokens. Iterative refinement: It makes multiple passes, locking in the correct tokens and using them as context to refine the rest. Final polish: The text converges into the finished output. Because the model can see the whole block while writing it, it self-corrects in real time and opens up patterns sequential models struggle with.  Think in-line editing, code infilling, near real-time code rendering, even non-linear structures like amino acid sequences or math graphs.  One fine-tune from Unsloth even taught it to solve Sudoku, a task that trips up left-to-right models because each answer depends on future ones. One honest trade-off.  To win on speed it gives up some quality, so Google still recommends standard Gemma 4 when you need maximum output quality, and the speedup is built for local and low-concurrency use rather than high-volume cloud serving.  This is a tool for researchers and developers exploring fast interactive local work, not a drop-in replacement for production. Weights are on Hugging Face today, with support across MLX, vLLM, Transformers and NVIDIA\'s stack. What would you run locally first if your own GPU could keep up? Everything else you shouldn’t miss: A $1.8T bet on a company that loses money: SpaceX\'s $75B IPO just valued a money-losing company at $1.8T and made Elon Musk the world\'s first trillionaire on the strength of orbital data centers and Mars bases that don\'t exist yet. Amazon\'s data centers drink less than you\'d think: Amazon says its data centers run over 7x more water-efficient than the industry average and return 3 gallons for every 4 used in 2025, putting it 75% of the way to being water-positive by 2030. Oracle beats earnings, sinks anyway: Oracle beat Q4 estimates with $19.2B in revenue and $2.03 adjusted EPS, then watched its stock drop roughly 10% after announcing a $40B raise to fund its AI data center buildout. Xbox braces for a July reset: Microsoft\'s Xbox is planning major July layoffs as new CEO Asha Sharma warns staff the business \"cannot continue\" on its current path. Learn more about AI from the experts building it 📸 Follow us on Instagram for fast, visual AI updates in 30 seconds.  🐝 Subscribe to our Generative AI daily newsletter — trusted by 120 000+ subscribers — to stay ahead of the latest AI news across tech, education, and business.  📺 Watch us on YouTube to hear insights directly from leading AI voices, builders, and innovators. 🐦 Follow us on X for breaking AI news and real-time industry updates. 🧠 Learn how to build your next AI application with practical resources and expert guidance. 🚀 Explore investment opportunities in the future of AI and join our community-backed growth journey. PS: Want to collaborate with the biggest GTM engine and distribution moat for AI companies? You can feature your tool with us and reach millions.\nKeep reading on LinkedIn\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/comm/pulse/claudes-fable-5-took-crypto-test-genai-works-r6fsf?lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BFiVqBTRTTpm%2BFpDHbqn2dg%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=3BZhvW8Ag30sk1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-newsletter_content_preview_text-0-readmore_button_-null-icq8ji~mqb6c5fn~7c-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mqb6c5fn-7c\n  \n      \n\n----------------------------------------\n\nThis email was intended for Yashica Lohar (Flipkart GWC 7.0 Scholar | CSE ’28 @ IIIT Jabalpur | 2 ⭐ @Codechef)\nLearn why we included this: https://www.linkedin.com/help/linkedin/answer/4788?lang=en&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BFiVqBTRTTpm%2BFpDHbqn2dg%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=3BZhvW8Ag30sk1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-SecurityHelp-0-textfooterglimmer-null-icq8ji~mqb6c5fn~7c-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mqb6c5fn-7c\nYou are receiving LinkedIn notification emails. Others can see that you are a subscriber.\n\n\nUnsubscribe: https://www.linkedin.com/series-notifications/?action=unsubscribe&memberToken=ADoAAEIlwb4B7wSObgzu320aKxhPrfHhU2UtxwY&newsletterId=7075090904906149891&newsletterTitle=The Atlas&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BFiVqBTRTTpm%2BFpDHbqn2dg%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=3BZhvW8Ag30sk1&ek=email_series_follow_newsletter_02&e=icq8ji-mqb6c5fn-7c&eid=icq8ji-mqb6c5fn-7c&m=unsubscribe&ts=footerGlimmer&li=0&t=plh    ·  Help: https://www.linkedin.com/help/linkedin/answer/67?lang=en&lipi=urn%3Ali%3Apage%3Aemail_email_series_follow_newsletter_02%3BFiVqBTRTTpm%2BFpDHbqn2dg%3D%3D&midToken=AQG-BFco0wt-tA&midSig=3BZhvW8Ag30sk1&trk=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-help-0-textfooterglimmer&trkEmail=eml-email_series_follow_newsletter_02-help-0-textfooterglimmer-null-icq8ji~mqb6c5fn~7c-null-null&eid=icq8ji-mqb6c5fn-7c\n\n© 2026 LinkedIn Corporation, 1zwnj000 West Maude Avenue, Sunnyvale, CA 94085.\nLinkedIn and the LinkedIn logo are registered trademarks of LinkedIn.','incoming-complaint','INCOMING',NULL,'2026-06-21 16:52:16',NULL,NULL,NULL),('e7322a35-6703-11f1-a09a-8107a00d61c0','e72ffad3-6703-11f1-a09a-8107a00d61c0',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Dear The Atlas,\n\nWe have received your ticket regarding: \"Claude\'s Fable 5 took the crypto test\".\n\nYour reference ID is #e72ffad3. This issue has been logged and is currently under review by our team.','approved-draft-placeholder','INCOMING',NULL,'2026-06-21 16:52:16',NULL,NULL,NULL),('e74a9981-6703-11f1-a09a-8107a00d61c0','e749a993-6703-11f1-a09a-8107a00d61c0',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[cid:image001.jpg@01DCFB21.9B6348E0]\nDear Yashica Lohar,\nGreetings from the ICPC AlgoQueen Team! 👑💻\nAs a registered participant of ICPC AlgoQueen 2026, you are getting exclusive access to a special speaker session designed to help you strengthen your competitive programming and problem-solving journey. 🚀\nWe are excited to invite you to our upcoming expert session:\n🎤 Speaker: Shinjan Chaturvedi\n💼 Designation: Software Engineer, Rubrik\n📚 Topic: Understanding Basic and a Few Advanced Tree Concepts to Solve Standard Problems\n📅 Date: 13th June 2026\n⏰ Time: 6:00 PM – 7:00 PM (IST)\n🔗 Session Link: https://teams.microsoft.com/meet/41997983284962?p=6wJcWnsk51SfEvT0zr\nTrees are among the most important data structures in competitive programming and technical interviews. In this session, participants will learn fundamental tree concepts and explore a few advanced techniques commonly used to solve standard problems efficiently. The session will also cover problem-solving approaches, practical insights, and strategies that can help participants strengthen their competitive programming skills.\nAn interactive Q&A session will be included, providing participants with an opportunity to engage directly with the speaker and clarify their doubts.\n✨ As part of AlgoQueen 2026, participants will also receive access to 10+ upcoming expert sessions featuring industry professionals, mentors, and experienced programmers.\n📲 Follow our official social media page for updates and announcements: @icpc_amrita_ on Instagram<https://www.instagram.com/icpc_amrita_/?utm_source=chatgpt.com>\n👉 Event Registration & Details: ICPC AlgoQueen 2026 Official Website<https://algoqueen.icpc.global/?utm_source=chatgpt.com>\nFor any queries, feel free to contact us at algoqueen@cb.amrita.edu<mailto:algoqueen@cb.amrita.edu>\nWe look forward to seeing you at the session! 🚀\nBest regards,\nICPC AlgoQueen Team 2026\nAmrita Vishwa Vidyapeetham | ICPC Foundation\n[Amrita University]\nDisclaimer: The information transmitted in this email, including attachments, is intended only for the person(s) or entity to which it is addressed and may contain confidential and/or privileged material. Any review, retransmission, dissemination or other use of, or taking of any action in reliance upon this information by persons or entities other than the intended recipient is prohibited. Any views expressed in any message are those of the individual sender and may not necessarily reflect the views of Amrita Vishwa Vidyapeetham. If you received this in error, please contact the sender and destroy any copies of this information.\n','incoming-complaint','INCOMING',NULL,'2026-06-21 16:52:16',NULL,NULL,NULL),('e74b4530-6703-11f1-a09a-8107a00d61c0','e749a993-6703-11f1-a09a-8107a00d61c0',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Dear ICPC Amritapuri,\n\nWe have received your ticket regarding: \"Exclusive Speaker Session on Tree Concepts – ICPC AlgoQueen 2026\".\n\nYour reference ID is #e749a993. This issue has been logged and is currently under review by our team.','approved-draft-placeholder','INCOMING',NULL,'2026-06-21 16:52:16',NULL,NULL,NULL),('e7565508-6703-11f1-a09a-8107a00d61c0','e754e5ff-6703-11f1-a09a-8107a00d61c0',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'[https://link.coursera.org/q/6PzJyylCV_utWAkZa9xHyg~~/AAQRxRA~/WcV_vqS1Jg7aXtEtBAWMqG54nTXShPGaUb5OseLvXBbvxXzmpsUUVOrF17o5uhjXsIahZgbSl5XXzDvrmxKbYA~~]\nMake AI your productivity superpower\n͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿\n͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿\n͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿\n͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿\n͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿\n͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿ ͏ ‌     ﻿\n͏ ‌   